plementation"},"3842":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-14","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#transport-modes","content":"`SELECTOR`"},"3843":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-15","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#transport-modes","content":"Non-blocking NIO with a dedicated selector thread and coroutine suspension."},"3844":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-16","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#transport-modes","content":"`SelectorConnection`"},"3845":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-17","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#transport-modes","content":"`VIRTUAL_THREADS`"},"3846":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-18","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#transport-modes","content":"Blocking sockets where each session runs on a virtual thread."},"3847":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-19","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#transport-modes","content":"`BlockingConnection`"},"3848":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-20","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#selectorloop-mechanics","content":"The `SelectorLoop` is the engine that turns NIO readiness into coroutine suspension. It operates on a dedicated thread to avoid deadlocks during `SelectionKey.register` calls on certain JDK implementations (`SelectorLoop.kt:48-55`)."},"3849":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-21","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#selectorloop-mechanics","content":"To ensure thread safety, interest changes are not applied directly from other threads. Instead, they are posted to a `ConcurrentLinkedQueue` and applied by the loop thread itself (`SelectorLoop.kt:94-96`). Every change triggers a `selector.wakeup()` to ensure the loop processes the pending actions immediately (`SelectorLoop.kt:95-96`)."},"3850":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-22","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#selectorconnection-and-suspension","content":"`SelectorConnection` implements the `Connection` interface by using the `SelectorLoop` to suspend coroutines when I/O is not ready. When `readFully` or `writeFully` encounters a zero-byte operation, it calls `loop.awaitReadable(key)` or `loop.awaitWritable(key)` (`Connection.kt:65-75`). This suspends the session coroutine, freeing up the thread until the `SelectorLoop` detects readiness and resumes the `CancellableContinuation` (`SelectorLoop.kt:80-81`)."},"3851":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-23","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#blockingconnection-and-virtual-threads","content":"`BlockingConnection` serves as the baseline for performance measurements (`Connection.kt:100-102`). While virtual threads make blocking code efficient, they do not virtualize disk I/O. Consequently, a `transferTo` call that triggers a page fault can pin the carrier thread to the disk, potentially stalling the executor (`Connection.kt:104-106`)."},"3852":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-24","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#partialframe-assembly","content":"The protocol requires full frames to be assembled from the stream. The `PartialFrameTest` verifies that the selector correctly handles frames split across multiple packets (`PartialFrameTest.kt:40-56`) and even frames delivered one byte at a time (`PartialFrameTest.kt:66-76`). This ensures the `SelectorConnection` correctly waits for more data via `loop.awaitReadable` when a `read` returns zero (`Connection.kt:66`)."},"3853":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-25","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#connection-failure-and-frame-corruption","content":"The server is designed to be resilient to malformed data. If a client sends an unserviceable request (e.g., an unsupported version), the broker responds with an `ErrorCode` but keeps the connection alive (`PartialFrameTest.kt:80-109`). However, an \"absurd\" frame length—such as a request claiming a massive size—is treated as a fatal error that drops the connection to protect the broker's memory (`PartialFrameTest.kt:112-129`)."},"3854":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-26","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#key-files","content":"File"},"3855":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-27","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#key-files","content":"Lines"},"3856":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-28","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#key-files","content":"What is there"},"3857":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-29","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#key-files","content":"`…/net/BooblikServer.kt`"},"3858":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-30","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#key-files","content":"`25-34`"},"3859":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-31","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#key-files","content":"`Transport` enum defining the two execution models."},"3860":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-32","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#key-files","content":"`…/nio/Connection.kt`"},"3861":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-33","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#key-files","content":"`55-142`"},"3862":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-34","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#key-files","content":"`Connection` interface and its two implementations."},"3863":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-35","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#key-files","content":"`…/nio/SelectorLoop.kt`"},"3864":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-36","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#key-files","content":"`31-41`"},"3865":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-37","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#key-files","content":"The dedicated thread and management of the `Selector`."},"3866":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-38","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#key-files","content":"`…/net/PartialFrameTest.kt`"},"3867":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-39","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#key-files","content":"`29-175`"},"3868":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-40","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#key-files","content":"Tests for split packets and frame assembly."},"3869":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-41","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Interest changes are asynchronous:** Because `SelectionKey.interestOps` can block or be ineffective during a `select()` call, `SelectorLoop` must queue changes and use `selector.wakeup()` to apply them safely (`SelectorLoop.kt:26-28`)."},"3870":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-42","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Disk I/O pins carrier threads:** Even when using virtual threads, `BlockingConnection` can cause a carrier thread to stall if `transferTo` encounters a page fault during disk I/O (`Connection.kt:104-106`)."},"3871":{"id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport-43","page_id":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/booblik-net/transport#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Zero-copy requires the raw channel:** Wrapping a `SocketChannel` in a decorator can silently turn a `sendfile` path into a copy loop, making `transferTarget` a critical property for performance (`Connection.kt:28-30`)."},"3872":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","type":"page","content":"Atomic offset persistence","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/common"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence"},"3873":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"3874":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#fileoffsetstore","content":"FileOffsetStore"},"3875":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#the-write-ahead-log-and-segmentmode","content":"The write-ahead-log and SegmentMode"},"3876":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#recovery-after-a-crash","content":"Recovery after a crash"},"3877":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#corruption-detection-and-checksums","content":"Corruption detection and checksums"},"3878":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#corruptiontest","content":"CorruptionTest"},"3879":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#key-files","content":"Key files"},"3880":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#behaviour-that-surprising","content":"Behaviour that surprising"},"3881":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"3882":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#fileoffsetstore","content":"The mechanism for persisting consumer positions using atomic file moves to prevent corruption during crashes. As described in `FileOffsetStore.kt:23-25`, a position saved halfway is considered worse than no position at all, as a truncated offset like `\"1\"` parsed from `\"12\"` would cause silent replays. To prevent this, the `save` method writes to a temporary file and uses `StandardCopyOption.ATOMIC_MOVE` to ensure the offset is updated completely or not at all `FileOffsetStore.kt:55-57`."},"3883":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#the-write-ahead-log-and-segmentmode","content":"The system provides two distinct write paths for log segments, which can be selected via the `SegmentMode` enum `LogSegment.kt:25`:"},"3884":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#the-write-ahead-log-and-segmentmode","content":"Mode"},"3885":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#the-write-ahead-log-and-segmentmode","content":"Description"},"3886":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#the-write-ahead-log-and-segmentmode","content":"`FILE_CHANNEL`"},"3887":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#the-write-ahead-log-and-segmentmode","content":"Uses a standard `FileChannel` for writing."},"3888":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#the-write-ahead-log-and-segmentmode","content":"`MAPPED`"},"3889":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#the-write-ahead-log-and-segmentmode","content":"Uses memory mapping for the write path."},"3890":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#the-write-ahead-log-and-segmentmode","content":"The `MAPPED` mode is the default and is chosen based on performance measurements `LogSegment.kt:19-20`. While `MAPPED` can be faster, it can suffer from performance degradation when the writer outruns the OS writeback, a phenomenon explored in `SustainedWriteProbe.kt:18-19`."},"3891":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#recovery-after-a-crash","content":"When a `LogSegment` is opened, it must undergo a recovery process to rebuild the `SparseOffsetIndex` by walking the record headers `LogSegment.kt:253-254`. The recovery mechanism reads headers in chunks using a `RECOVERY_BUFFER` to avoid excessive syscalls `LogSegment.kt:327-330`. Because recovery relies on the length prefix, a record whose declared length extends beyond the file boundary is discarded, and the segment is truncated to the last intact boundary `LogSegment.kt:302-304`."},"3892":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#corruption-detection-and-checksums","content":"To address the risk where a torn write inside a record body is not detectable by length prefixes alone, the system implements checksums (M-60) `LogSegment.kt:258-259`. During recovery, the `verify` function checks the record's body against its stored CRC `LogSegment.kt:405`. If a mismatch is detected, the recovery process stops to prevent the system from treating garbage as valid data `LogSegment.kt:364-366`."},"3893":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#corruptiontest","content":"The `CorruptionTest` class validates that the system correctly identifies and reacts to data corruption `CorruptionTest.kt:23`. Key test cases include:"},"3894":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#corruptiontest","content":"**Bit-flipping**: Ensuring a single bit flip in a record body is caught `CorruptionTest.kt:50-51`."},"3895":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#corruptiontest","content":"**Torn bodies**: Verifying that even if a length prefix survives, a modified body is detected `CorruptionTest.kt:108-109`."},"3896":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#corruptiontest","content":"**Recovery boundaries**: Ensuring that once a bad record is encountered, the system stops and does not allow subsequent records to be incorrectly processed `CorruptionTest.kt:64-65`."},"3897":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#key-files","content":"File"},"3898":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#key-files","content":"Lines"},"3899":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#key-files","content":"What is there"},"3900":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#key-files","content":"`…/common/FileOffsetStore.kt`"},"3901":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#key-files","content":"`27-29`"},"3902":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#key-files","content":"The `FileOffsetStore` class for atomic offset persistence."},"3903":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-30","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#key-files","content":"`…/storage/LogSegment.kt`"},"3904":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-31","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#key-files","content":"`24-25`"},"3905":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-32","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#key-files","content":"The `SegmentMode` enumeration."},"3906":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-33","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#key-files","content":"`…/storage/LogSegment.kt`"},"3907":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-34","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#key-files","content":"`261-267`"},"3908":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-35","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#key-files","content":"The `open` function for initializing a segment."},"3909":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-36","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#key-files","content":"`…/storage/CorruptionTest.kt`"},"3910":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-37","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#key-files","content":"`49-51`"},"3911":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-38","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#key-files","content":"Test case for bit-flipping detection."},"3912":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-39","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#behaviour-that-surprising","content":"The `LogSegment.open` function performs a `recover` operation that skips record bodies, only reading headers to rebuild the index `LogSegment.kt:319`."},"3913":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-40","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#behaviour-that-surprising","content":"In `FileOffsetStore`, the `save` operation is designed to be \"all or nothing\" by using a temporary file and an atomic move to prevent partial writes of the offset `FileOffsetStore.kt:55-57`."},"3914":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence-41","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/atomic-offset-persistence#behaviour-that-surprising","content":"The `SustainedWriteProbe` is designed to intentionally saturate the OS writeback to observe the performance degradation of the `MAPPED` mode `SustainedWriteProbe.kt:32-33`."},"3915":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","type":"page","content":"FileOffsetStore","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/common"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore"},"3916":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"What this module is responsible for"},"3917":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"3918":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore#fileoffsetstore","content":"FileOffsetStore"},"3919":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore#atomic-move-via-temporary-files","content":"Atomic move via temporary files"},"3920":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore#at-least-once-checkpointing","content":"At-least-once checkpointing"},"3921":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore#file-based-position-recovery","content":"File-based position recovery"},"3922":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore#key-files","content":"Key files"},"3923":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"3924":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"3925":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"The `FileOffsetStore` provides a simple implementation of the `OffsetStore` interface, designed for consumers that need to persist their position in a log on a local volume. It ensures that a consumer's position outlives the process by storing the `Offset` in a file, allowing for recovery after a restart `FileOffsetStore.kt:27-29`."},"3926":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore#fileoffsetstore","content":"The `FileOffsetStore` is a concrete implementation of `OffsetStore` that uses the local file system to track progress. It maps a combination of `TopicName` and `PartitionId` to a specific file path within a configured directory `FileOffsetStore.kt:61-64`."},"3927":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore#atomic-move-via-temporary-files","content":"To prevent data corruption, the `save` operation does not overwrite the existing offset file directly. Instead, it writes the new offset to a sibling file with a `.tmp` extension and then performs an atomic move using `StandardCopyOption.ATOMIC_MOVE` `FileOffsetStore.kt:55-57`. This ensures that a partially written file (e.g., a truncated `\"12\"` becoming `\"1\"`) does not result in a valid but incorrect offset that could cause silent record replays `FileOffsetStore.kt:23-25`."},"3928":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore#at-least-once-checkpointing","content":"The system guarantees at-least-once delivery by performing `checkpointing` only **after** the collector has successfully handled a batch of records `README.md:35-36`. If a failure occurs between the processing of the batch and the call to `save`, the consumer will restart from the previous offset and replay the batch `README.md:36`."},"3929":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore#file-based-position-recovery","content":"When a consumer restarts, it uses the `load` function to retrieve the last known `Offset` from the file system `FileOffsetStore.kt:34-46`. Because the broker does not store consumer positions, the responsibility for maintaining this state lies entirely with the client/consumer `feature-subscribe-and-publish.md:30-31`."},"3930":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore#key-files","content":"File"},"3931":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore#key-files","content":"Lines"},"3932":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore#key-files","content":"What is there"},"3933":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore#key-files","content":"`…/common/FileOffsetStore.kt`"},"3934":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore#key-files","content":"`27-64`"},"3935":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore#key-files","content":"Implementation of `FileOffsetStore` including `load`, `save`, and file path resolution."},"3936":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"The `save` method uses a temporary file and `StandardCopyOption.ATOMIC_MOVE` to ensure that a position saved halfway through a write does not result in a truncated offset `FileOffsetStore.kt:55-57`."},"3937":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"`checkpointing` is designed for at-least-once semantics; saving the offset before handling the batch would result in at-most-once delivery, potentially skipping records `README.md:35-36`."},"3938":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common/fileoffsetstore#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"The `load` function returns `null` if the offset file does not exist, which is treated as a valid starting point for a new consumer `FileOffsetStore.kt:40`."},"3939":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common","type":"page","content":"dev/common","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/common"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common"},"3940":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"3941":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-common#fileoffsetstore","content":"FileOffsetStore"},"3942":{"id":"/w