"page_id":"/wiki/dev-common","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common#load-and-save-operations","content":"The lifecycle of reading and writing offsets for a specific TopicName and PartitionId, handled by the `load` and `save` methods in `FileOffsetStore.kt:34-58`."},"3953":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common#key-files","content":"File"},"3954":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common#key-files","content":"Lines"},"3955":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common#key-files","content":"What is there"},"3956":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common#key-files","content":"`…/common/FileOffsetStore.kt`"},"3957":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common#key-files","content":"`27-64`"},"3958":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common#key-files","content":"The implementation of the `FileOffsetStore` class and its file-based logic."},"3959":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common#public-api","content":"What"},"3960":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common#public-api","content":"Where"},"3961":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common#public-api","content":"Why"},"3962":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common#public-api","content":"`FileOffsetStore`"},"3963":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common#public-api","content":"`FileOffsetStore.kt:27`"},"3964":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common#public-api","content":"Provides a filesystem-based implementation of the `OffsetStore` interface."},"3965":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"`save` uses a temporary file and an atomic move (`FileOffsetStore.kt:55-57`) to ensure that a partially written offset does not corrupt the state."},"3966":{"id":"/wiki/dev-common-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev-common","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-common#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"`load` returns `null` if the offset file does not exist on the disk (`FileOffsetStore.kt:40`)."},"3967":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","type":"page","content":"consumeForever","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/consumer"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever"},"3968":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"What this module is responsible for"},"3969":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"3970":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#the-consumeforever-lifecycle","content":"The `consumeForever` lifecycle"},"3971":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#at-least-once-delivery-via-checkpointing","content":"At-least-once delivery via `checkpointing`"},"3972":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#recovery-from-broker-disconnection","content":"Recovery from broker disconnection"},"3973":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#the-stats-snapshot-mechanism","content":"The `Stats` snapshot mechanism"},"3974":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"Key files"},"3975":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"Behaviour that surprise"},"3976":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"3977":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"The `consumeForever` function serves as the primary execution engine for the consumer service. It orchestrates the continuous reading of a specific partition from a Booblik topic, manages the lifecycle of the network connection to the broker, and ensures that the consumer's progress is persisted to local storage to allow for seamless restarts."},"3978":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#the-consumeforever-lifecycle","content":"The lifecycle begins by initializing a `FileOffsetStore` (`Main.kt:60`) and determining the starting position for the stream. As seen in `Main.kt:60-68`, the consumer attempts to load a previously saved offset from the `stateDir`; if no state exists, it defaults to `StartPosition.Earliest`. Once the starting position is determined, the function enters an infinite `while(true)` loop (`Main.kt:75`) that manages the `BooblikSubscriber` connection. The subscriber uses the `follow` method (`Subscription.kt:120`) to stream `RecordBatch` objects from the broker to the consumer."},"3979":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#at-least-once-delivery-via-checkpointing","content":"Data integrity is maintained through a specific execution order in the `checkpointing` operator. As implemented in `Subscription.kt:260-265`, the `checkpointing` extension function wraps the collection process. It ensures that the `store.save` operation occurs only **after** the `emit(batch)` call has successfully passed the data to the collector. This ensures that if the `handle` function (`Main.kt:100-107`) fails or the process crashes, the offset is not updated, causing the next run to replay the same batch, thus guaranteeing at-least-once delivery."},"3980":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#recovery-from-broker-disconnection","content":"The consumer is designed to be resilient to network instability. If the connection to the broker is lost, the `BooblikSubscriber` throws an exception which is caught by the `try-catch` block in `Main.kt:89-95`. Instead of terminating, the loop logs the failure, increments the `reconnects` metric in the `Stats` object (`Main.kt:93`), waits for 1000ms (`Main.kt:94`), and attempts to re-establish the connection. Upon reconnection, the consumer uses the last successfully saved offset via `StartPosition.At(it)` (`Main.kt:68`) to resume reading exactly where it left off."},"3981":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#the-stats-snapshot-mechanism","content":"Real-time observability is provided by the `Stats` class, which maintains the current state of the consumer. The `observe` function (`Main.kt:125-131`) is called for every batch to update the `position` (the next expected offset) and the `lag` (the distance from the high watermark). These values, along with the `lastRecord` and `reconnects` count, are captured in a `ConsumerStats` data class via the `snapshot` method (`Main.kt:133-144`) to be served over HTTP."},"3982":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"File"},"3983":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"Lines"},"3984":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"What is there"},"3985":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"`…/consumer/Main.kt`"},"3986":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"`56-97`"},"3987":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"The `consumeForever` orchestration loop and error handling."},"3988":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"`…/consumer/Main.kt`"},"3989":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"`109-145`"},"3990":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"The `Stats` class and `ConsumerStats` data structure."},"3991":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"`…/client/Subscription.kt`"},"3992":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"`260-265`"},"3993":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#key-files","content":"The `checkpointing` extension function for at-least-once delivery."},"3994":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"**At-least-once semantics**: Because `checkpointing` saves the offset *after* the batch is emitted to the collector (`Subscription.kt:263`), a crash during the `handle` function will result in the same batch being processed again upon restart."},"3995":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"**Connection ownership**: A `BooblikSubscriber` owns its own connection per partition; if you follow multiple partitions, `BooblikSubscriber` will open a separate connection for each to avoid head-of-line blocking (`Subscription.kt:157-158`)."},"3996":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/consumeforever#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"**Non-blocking health checks**: The `Health` check does not use a simple TCP connect because a successful handshake does not guarantee the broker is actually processing requests; instead, it performs a full `metadata` request (`Health.kt:60-61`)."},"3997":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","type":"page","content":"dev/consumer","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/consumer"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer"},"3998":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"3999":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"ConsumerConfig"},"4000":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumeforever","content":"consumeForever"},"4001":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#recovery-after-a-crash","content":"Recovery after a crash"},"4002":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#reconnection-logic","content":"Reconnection logic"},"4003":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#stats-and-monitoring","content":"Stats and monitoring"},"4004":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"Key files"},"4005":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"Behaviour that surprise"},"4006":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-16, sources: 2. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"4007":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"Configuration parameters are loaded from environment variables to define how the consumer connects to the broker and where it stores its state, as implemented in `Main.kt:160-181`."},"4008":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"Environment Variable"},"4009":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"Default Value"},"4010":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"Description"},"4011":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`CONSUMER_NAME`"},"4012":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`consumer`"},"4013":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"Name of the consumer instance"},"4014":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`BOOBLIK_HOST`"},"4015":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`127.0.0.1`"},"4016":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"Broker host address"},"4017":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`BOOBLIK_PORT`"},"4018":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`9092`"},"4019":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"Broker port"},"4020":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`BOOBLIK_TOPIC`"},"4021":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`events`"},"4022":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"The topic to subscribe to"},"4023":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`BOOBLIK_PARTITION`"},"4024":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`0`"},"4025":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"The specific partition to consume"},"4026":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`STATE_DIR`"},"4027":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`/var/lib/consumer`"},"4028":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-30","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"Directory for `FileOffsetStore`"},"4029":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-31","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`HTTP_PORT`"},"4030":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-32","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"`8080`"},"4031":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-33","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumerconfig","content":"Port for the metrics HTTP server"},"4032":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-34","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumeforever","content":"The main execution loop, implemented in `Main.kt:56-97`, maintains a continuous connection to the broker. It uses a `while(true)` loop to ensure that if a `BooblikSubscriber` fails or the broker becomes unavailable, the consumer attempts to re-establish the subscription."},"4033":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-35","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#consumeforever","content":"More: consumeForever"},"4034":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-36","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#recovery-after-a-crash","content":"To ensure at-least-once delivery, the consumer uses `FileOffsetStore` to persist the last successfully processed offset. Upon startup, `consumeForever` checks for a saved offset in `Main.kt:67-68`; if found, it resumes from that position; otherwise, it starts from `StartPosition.Earliest`. The checkpointing mechanism is applied via `.checkpointing(store)` in `Main.kt:83`, ensuring the offset is saved only after the batch has been processed."},"4035":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-37","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#reconnection-logic","content":"Connectivity issues are handled within a `try-catch` block inside the loop in `Main.kt:76-95`. If an exception occurs (such as the broker going away), the consumer increments the `reconnects` counter, waits for a 1000ms delay, and then attempts to reconnect."},"4036":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-38","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#reconnection-logic","content":"More: Reconnection logic"},"4037":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-39","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#stats-and-monitoring","content":"The service exposes operational metrics through an embedded Ktor server configured in `Main.kt:46-52`. The `Stats` class tracks the number of handled records, the current position, the lag, and the number of reconnection attempts, which are then serialized to JSON via the `/stats` endpoint."},"4038":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-40","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"File"},"4039":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-41","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"Lines"},"4040":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-42","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"What is there"},"4041":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-43","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"`…/consumer/build.gradle.kts`"},"4042":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-44","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"`1-8`"},"4043":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-45","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"Dependency declarations for Ktor, Booblik client, and serialization"},"4044":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-46","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"`…/consumer/Main.kt`"},"4045":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-47","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"`38-54`"},"4046":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-48","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"The `main` function entry point and server setup"},"4047":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-49","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"`…/consumer/Main.kt`"},"4048":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-50","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"`56-97`"},"4049":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-51","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"The `consumeForever` loop and subscription logic"},"4050":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-52","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"`…/consumer/Main.kt`"},"4051":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-53","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"`109-145`"},"4052":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-54","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"The `Stats` class and `ConsumerStats` data class"},"4053":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-55","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"`…/consumer/Main.kt`"},"4054":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-56","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"`160-181`"},"4055":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-57","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#key-files","content":"The `ConsumerConfig` data class and environment loading"},"4056":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-58","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"The `checkpointing` function in `consumeForever` is designed such that the offset is saved **after** the `collect` block finishes, which guarantees at-least-once delivery by replaying batches if a crash occurs before the save is completed (`Main.kt:80-82`)."},"4057":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer-59","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"The `StartPosition.Earliest` setting used in `consumeForever` refers to the start of the **live** log (the data currently retained by the broker) rather than the absolute beginning of the log history (`Main.kt:64-66`)."},"4058":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","type":"page","content":"Reconnection logic","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/consumer"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic"},"4059":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"4060":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#the-boobliksubscriber-lifecycle","content":"The `BooblikSubscriber` lifecycle"},"4061":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#the-booblikconnection-and-session-failure-modes","content":"The `BooblikConnection` and `Session` failure modes"},"4062":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#the-lastacceptfailure-and-lastsessionfailure-metrics","content":"The `lastAcceptFailure` and `lastSessionFailure` metrics"},"4063":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#the-sessionstresstest-pipelined-exchange","content":"The `SessionStressTest` pipelined exchange"},"4064":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"Key files"},"4065":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"4066":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-16, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"4067":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#the-boobliksubscriber-lifecycle","content":"The consumer's main loop is designed to be resilient to broker unavailability. As seen in `Main.kt:75-96`, the `while(true)` loop wraps the entire `BooblikSubscriber` lifecycle. If the broker goes away, the `catch (failure: Exception)` block intercepts the error, increments the `reconnects` counter in the `Stats` class (`Main.kt:93`), and waits for 1000ms before attempting to re-establish the connection. This ensures that a broker restart does not crash the consumer service, as the consumer maintains its state via the `FileOffsetStore` (`Main.kt:60`)."},"4068":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#the-booblikconnection-and-session-failure-modes","content":"The system distinguishes between different levels of failure to provide better diagnostic data. While `Consumer.kt:89-91` shows that a `poll()` can throw a `FetchFailedException` when the broker returns a non-zero `ErrorCode`, there are deeper failures. A `Session` might die due to an exception while a request is being handled, which is a distinct event from a client simply leaving a connection. This distinction is critical for identifying bugs in the server's request handling logic (`Metrics.kt:55-67`)."},"4069":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#the-lastacceptfailure-and-lastsessionfailure-metrics","content":"The broker provides specific metrics to help operators distinguish between \"the broker is unreachable\" and \"the broker is running but failing to handle requests.\" According to `Metrics.kt:45-67`, the `lastAcceptFailure` captures exceptions occurring in the `accept` loop, which can lead to a \"silent death\" where the process is alive but no new connections can be made. Meanwhile, `lastSessionFailure` captures exceptions that kill a specific session while it is actively holding a request, providing evidence for intermittent failures that would otherwise be invisible (`Metrics.kt:55-67`)."},"4070":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#the-sessionstresstest-pipelined-exchange","content":"To ensure the stability of the pipelined exchange, `SessionStressTest.kt:27-52` runs a high-volume loop of produce and receive operations. This test is designed to catch rare, non-deterministic failures (like the \"M-64\" issue) by repeating the exchange hundreds of times. A key concern during these tests is ephemeral port exhaustion; if the test environment runs out of local ports due to many connections stuck in `TIME_WAIT`, the test will fail not because of a broker bug, but because the harness has exhausted the OS resources (`SessionStressTest.kt:97-101`)."},"4071":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"File"},"4072":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"Lines"},"4073":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"What is there"},"4074":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"`…/consumer/Main.kt`"},"4075":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"`75-96`"},"4076":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"The infinite retry loop and reconnection delay logic."},"4077":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"`…/net/Metrics.kt`"},"4078":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"`45-67`"},"4079":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"Volatile fields for tracking the last accept and session failures."},"4080":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"`…/client/Consumer.kt`"},"4081":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"`89-91`"},"4082":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"Logic for throwing `FetchFailedException` on broker errors."},"4083":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"`…/net/SessionStressTest.kt`"},"4084":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"`27-52`"},"4085":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#key-files","content":"The stress test harness for repeated connection cycles."},"4086":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Silent Accept Failures**: If an exception occurs in the `accept` loop, the broker might continue to run and hold its port, but it will stop accepting any new connections. This is tracked via `lastAcceptFailure` in `Metrics.kt:50`."},"4087":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**At-Least-Once via Checkpointing**: The `checkpointing` mechanism in `Main.kt:83` is intentionally placed after the `collect` block. This means if a crash occurs after processing a batch but before saving the offset, the batch will be replayed upon restart."},"4088":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/reconnection-logic#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**The `RecordExceedsMaxBytesException`**: A consumer might stop making progress if a single record is larger than the `maxBytes` configured in `Consumer.kt:28-35`. This is a client-side limit that results in a permanent failure to advance the position."},"4089":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","type":"page","content":"Recovery after a crash","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/consumer"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash"},"4090":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"4091":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#fileoffsetstore-and-atomic-position-updates","content":"FileOffsetStore and atomic position updates"},"4092":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#logsegment-recovery-and-index-rebuilding","content":"LogSegment recovery and index rebuilding"},"4093":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#segmentmode-and-data-integrity-boundaries","content":"SegmentMode and data integrity boundaries"},"4094":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#partitionlog-segment-continuity","content":"PartitionLog segment continuity"},"4095":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#the-at-least-once-guarantee-in-consumer-and-relay","content":"The at-least-once guarantee in consumer and relay"},"4096":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#validation-of-resumed-positions","content":"Validation of resumed positions"},"4097":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"Key files"},"4098":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"4099":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"4100":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#fileoffsetstore-and-atomic-position-updates","content":"To prevent corruption of the consumer's state, `FileOffsetStore` avoids overwriting the current offset file directly. Instead, it writes the new offset to a temporary file and uses an atomic move to replace the original, ensuring that a crash during the write process does not leave a truncated or corrupted offset file (`FileOffsetStore.kt:55-57`)."},"4101":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#logsegment-recovery-and-index-rebuilding","content":"When a broker restarts, it must reconstruct its internal lookup structures. The `LogSegment.open` function allows the system to reopen existing segments, verifying that the `nextOffset` and the index are consistent with the data stored on disk (`RecoveryTest.kt:37-38`)."},"4102":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#segmentmode-and-data-integrity-boundaries","content":"The behavior of the recovery depends on the `SegmentMode` used:"},"4103":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#segmentmode-and-data-integrity-boundaries","content":"Mode"},"4104":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#segmentmode-and-data-integrity-boundaries","content":"Recovery Behavior"},"4105":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#segmentmode-and-data-integrity-boundaries","content":"`FILE_CHANNEL`"},"4106":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#segmentmode-and-data-integrity-boundaries","content":"The file length acts as a boundary; a record with a header claiming more bytes than the file contains is discarded (`RecoveryTest.kt:78-79`)."},"4107":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#segmentmode-and-data-integrity-boundaries","content":"`MAPPED`"},"4108":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#segmentmode-and-data-integrity-boundaries","content":"The file length is pre-sized, so recovery relies on the presence of a zero-length prefix to identify the end of the log (`RecoveryTest.kt:100-101`)."},"4109":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#partitionlog-segment-continuity","content":"A `PartitionLog` is composed of multiple segments. Upon reopening, the system restores the continuity of the partition by identifying all existing segments and maintaining their order, ensuring the `nextOffset` reflects the total count across all segments (`RecoveryTest.kt:117-118`)."},"4110":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#the-at-least-once-guarantee-in-consumer-and-relay","content":"The system provides at-least-once delivery by ensuring that the position is only updated after the work is completed. In `consumeForever`, the `checkpointing` operator saves the offset only after the `collect` block (which includes `handle`) has finished (`Main.kt:83-84`). Similarly, the relay ensures that Kafka is flushed before the booblik position is updated (`Directions.kt:119`)."},"4111":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#validation-of-resumed-positions","content":"The system distinguishes between a successful recovery and a failure based on the starting point. A consumer is considered to have failed if it starts from the beginning of the log (`README.md:37-38`), whereas resuming from a position slightly behind the last known offset is considered a valid replay of a batch (`check.py:54-55`)."},"4112":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"File"},"4113":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"Lines"},"4114":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"What is there"},"4115":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"`…/common/FileOffsetStore.kt`"},"4116":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"`55-57`"},"4117":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"The atomic move mechanism using a temporary file."},"4118":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"`…/consumer/Main.kt`"},"4119":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"`83-84`"},"4120":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-30","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"The checkpointing logic that ensures at-least-once delivery."},"4121":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-31","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"`…/relay/Directions.kt`"},"4122":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-32","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"`119`"},"4123":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-33","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#key-files","content":"The requirement to flush Kafka before checkpointing."},"4124":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-34","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"`FileOffsetStore.save` uses a `.tmp` file to ensure that a crash during a write doesn't leave a \"half-saved\" position that could cause silent replays (`FileOffsetStore.kt:55-57`)."},"4125":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-35","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"In `booblikToKafka`, the relay does not store the Kafka key; while it preserves per-key ordering by using the key to pick the booblik partition, the key itself is lost during the crossing (`Directions.kt:115-116`)."},"4126":{"id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash-36","page_id":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-consumer/recovery-after-crash#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"The `check.py` script considers a consumer \"resumed\" even if it starts slightly behind its last known position, because replaying a batch is the expected behavior of an at-least-once system (`check.py:54-55`)."},"4127":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","type":"page","content":"dev/projection","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/projection"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection"},"4128":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"4129":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection#projection","content":"Projection"},"4130":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection#the-build-lifecycle","content":"The build lifecycle"},"4131":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection#progress","content":"Progress"},"4132":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection#recovery-after-a-broker-failure","content":"Recovery after a broker failure"},"4133":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"Key files"},"4134":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection#behaviour-that-should-surprise","content":"Behaviour that should surprise"},"4135":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 3. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"4136":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#projection","content":"The core in-memory state machine that derives `UserView` from the event log. The `Projection` class manages a `ConcurrentHashMap` of users and uses `AtomicLong` to track applied and skipped events `View.kt:39-43`. It processes `Event` objects by updating user-specific statistics like event counts and action frequencies `View.kt:55-67`."},"4137":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#projection","content":"More: Projection"},"4138":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#the-build-lifecycle","content":"The transition from the replay stage to the follow stage to ensure a gapless view. The `build` function first executes a `replay` from `StartPosition.Earliest` to catch up with history `Main.kt:98`. Once the replay completes, the `progress.replayComplete` flag is set to true `Main.kt:105`, and the system transitions to a `follow` stage for each partition `Main.kt:112-130`. This ensures that the `follow` starts exactly at the `nextOffset` reported by the replay `Main.kt:117-119`."},"4139":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#the-build-lifecycle","content":"More: The build lifecycle"},"4140":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#progress","content":"Monitoring metrics including replay/follow counts, rebuild counts, and partition positions. The `Progress` class tracks the number of events from replay and follow stages using `AtomicLong` `Main.kt:146-147`, and maintains a map of current partition positions `Main.kt:149`."},"4141":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#recovery-after-a-broker-failure","content":"The mechanism for rebuilding the entire state from the beginning of the log when the connection is lost. If an exception occurs during the build loop, the `catch` block increments the rebuild count and resets the `live` and `replayComplete` flags `Main.kt:135-140`. The entire process then restarts, re-reading the log from the beginning to ensure state consistency `Main.kt:87-143`."},"4142":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"File"},"4143":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"Lines"},"4144":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"What is there"},"4145":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"`…/projection/build.gradle.kts`"},"4146":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"`1-7`"},"4147":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"Dependencies for Ktor server, CIO, and serialization"},"4148":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"`…/projection/Main.kt`"},"4149":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"`44-77`"},"4150":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"The `main` function setting up the embedded server and routes"},"4151":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"`…/projection/Main.kt`"},"4152":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"`79-143`"},"4153":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"The `build` function containing the replay and follow logic"},"4154":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"`…/projection/Main.kt`"},"4155":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"`145-173`"},"4156":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"The `Progress` class for tracking internal metrics"},"4157":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"`…/projection/View.kt`"},"4158":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-30","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"`9-22`"},"4159":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-31","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"Data classes for `Event` and `UserView`"},"4160":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-32","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"`…/projection/View.kt`"},"4161":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-33","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"`39-77`"},"4162":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-34","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#key-files","content":"The `Projection` class implementing the state machine"},"4163":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-35","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#behaviour-that-should-surprise","content":"The `Projection` class does not persist its state to disk; instead, it relies on the fact that it can rebuild the entire state by replaying the log from the beginning `View.kt:25-32`."},"4164":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-36","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#behaviour-that-should-surprise","content":"If a record cannot be decoded, the `apply` function increments the `skipped` counter but continues processing the log rather than stopping `View.kt:49-53`."},"4165":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection-37","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection#behaviour-that-should-surprise","content":"The `follow` stage uses a separate `CoroutineScope` for each partition to allow concurrent consumption of partition streams `Main.kt:111-130`."},"4166":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","type":"page","content":"Projection","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/projection"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection"},"4167":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"4168":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#the-projection-lifecycle","content":"The Projection lifecycle"},"4169":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#the-projection-state-machine","content":"The Projection state machine"},"4170":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#recovery-after-a-crash","content":"Recovery after a crash"},"4171":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#verification-of-the-read-model","content":"Verification of the read model"},"4172":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#key-files","content":"Key files"},"4173":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#behaviour-that-surprising","content":"Behaviour that surprising"},"4174":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"4175":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","content":"Documentation for the Projection module, focusing on the read model implementation and its verification."},"4176":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#the-projection-lifecycle","content":"The lifecycle of a projection is split into two distinct phases to ensure the view is both complete and current. It begins with a `replay()` phase that reads from the `Earliest` position to build the initial state, and transitions to a `follow()` phase once the history has been exhausted `Main.kt:98-110`."},"4177":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#the-projection-state-machine","content":"The state is managed within the `Projection` class, where the `apply` function performs a functional fold over the incoming stream `View.kt:44-67`. The `UserView` is updated by merging new actions into the existing map and incrementing event counts. Crucially, the correctness of `lastAction` depends on the fact that all events for a single user are routed to the same partition, ensuring they arrive in order `View.kt:34-37`."},"4178":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#recovery-after-a-crash","content":"The design follows a \"persist neither\" philosophy to avoid silent corruption. Because the `Projection` does not persist its position to a volume, a restart forces a complete rebuild from the beginning of the log `View.kt:27-32`. This ensures that the view is always a complete reflection of the log, rather than a potentially truncated view that resumes from a stale offset `README.md:140-144`."},"4179":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#verification-of-the-read-model","content":"The verification suite, primarily implemented in `dev/check-projection.sh`, asserts several invariants:"},"4180":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#verification-of-the-read-model","content":"**Rebuild Completeness**: After a restart, the `applied` count must be at least as large as the count before the restart `check.py:204-207`."},"4181":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#verification-of-the-read-model","content":"**View Consistency**: The sum of all `events` across all users must exactly match the total number of `applied` events `check.py:181-184`."},"4182":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#verification-of-the-read-model","content":"**User Integrity**: For any specific user, the sum of counts in the `actions` map must equal the total `events` count for that user `check.py:224-226`."},"4183":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#key-files","content":"File"},"4184":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#key-files","content":"Lines"},"4185":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#key-files","content":"What is there"},"4186":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#key-files","content":"`…/projection/View.kt`"},"4187":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#key-files","content":"`10-22`"},"4188":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#key-files","content":"`Event` and `UserView` data classes"},"4189":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#key-files","content":"`…/projection/View.kt`"},"4190":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#key-files","content":"`39-77`"},"4191":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#key-files","content":"`Projection` class containing the state and `apply` logic"},"4192":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#key-files","content":"`…/projection/Main.kt`"},"4193":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#key-files","content":"`44-77`"},"4194":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#key-files","content":"`main` function setting up the Ktor server and routes"},"4195":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#key-files","content":"`…/projection/Main.kt`"},"4196":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#key-files","content":"`79-143`"},"4197":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-30","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#key-files","content":"`build` function managing the replay and follow loops"},"4198":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-31","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#key-files","content":"`…/projection/Main.kt`"},"4199":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-32","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#key-files","content":"`145-188`"},"4200":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-33","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#key-files","content":"`Progress` and `ProjectionStats` for monitoring"},"4201":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-34","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#behaviour-that-surprising","content":"The `apply` function in `Projection` uses `ConcurrentHashMap.compute` to ensure thread-safe updates to user views during the `follow` phase `View.kt:55`."},"4202":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-35","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#behaviour-that-surprising","content":"The `decode` function in `View.kt` uses `runCatching` to silently skip unreadable records, incrementing a `skipped` counter instead of crashing the projection `View.kt:48-53`."},"4203":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection-36","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/projection#behaviour-that-surprising","content":"The `top` function in `Projection` performs a full sort of all users in memory to return the requested limit `View.kt:71`."},"4204":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","type":"page","content":"The build lifecycle","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/projection"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle"},"4205":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"What this module is responsible for"},"4206":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"4207":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#the-replay-and-follow-transition","content":"The replay and follow transition"},"4208":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#the-statelessness-of-projection","content":"The statelessness of Projection"},"4209":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#rebuild-verification","content":"Rebuild verification"},"4210":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#at-least-once-semantics-in-checkpointing","content":"At-least-once semantics in checkpointing"},"4211":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#key-files","content":"Key files"},"4212":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"4213":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"4214":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"The `dev/projection` module implements a read model where state is a pure function of the event log. It is responsible for consuming a stream of events from a `BooblikSubscriber`, building an in-memory view, and providing an HTTP query surface."},"4215":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#the-replay-and-follow-transition","content":"To ensure the query surface transitions from \"building\" to \"current\" without losing or duplicating data, the projection uses a two-phase approach. First, it performs a `replay()` to consume history. The `RecordBatch.nextOffset` property is critical here; it reports where each partition stopped during the replay, allowing the subsequent `follow()` phase to start exactly at that offset `Main.kt:137-139`. This mechanism ensures that the view catches up to the high watermark without gaps or redundant processing."},"4216":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#the-statelessness-of-projection","content":"A core design principle is that the projection stores nothing on disk. While a consumer might persist its position, the projection's state is purely in-memory. As noted in the documentation, persisting a position without the corresponding state would lead to silent corruption `README.md:141-144`. Instead, upon any restart, the service must rebuild its entire state by replaying the log from the beginning to ensure the view is consistent with the truth of the log."},"4217":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#rebuild-verification","content":"The integrity of the rebuild process is verified by ensuring that a restarted service recovers at least the same amount of data it held previously. The `check-projection.sh` script performs this by capturing the `applied` count before a restart and asserting that the rebuilt view contains at least that many events `check-projection.sh:42-47`. This prevents a scenario where a service resumes from a late offset with an empty view, which would result in a \"successful\" but incorrect state."},"4218":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#at-least-once-semantics-in-checkpointing","content":"The system guarantees at-least-once delivery through the mechanics of `checkpointing`. The offset is only saved after the collector has successfully handled a batch of records `SubscriptionTest.kt:143-172`. If an error occurs during the `apply` phase, the checkpoint is not updated, meaning the next attempt will replay the same batch, ensuring no event is lost at the cost of potential duplicates."},"4219":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#key-files","content":"File"},"4220":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#key-files","content":"Lines"},"4221":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#key-files","content":"What is there"},"4222":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`…/projection/Main.kt`"},"4223":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`145-173`"},"4224":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#key-files","content":"The `Progress` class tracking replay/follow status and statistics."},"4225":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`dev/README.md`"},"4226":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`129-135`"},"4227":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#key-files","content":"Explanation of the `replay()` and `follow()` mechanics."},"4228":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`dev/check-projection.sh`"},"4229":{"id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-projection/the-build-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`42-47`"},"4230":{"id":"/wiki/dev-p