nection","content":"More: openConnection"},"4281":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher-40","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher#publishforever","content":"The main event loop that simulates user activity by hashing user keys to specific partitions and sending JSON payloads, which includes recording stats for each sent record in `Main.kt:88-108`."},"4282":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher-41","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher#publishtasks","content":"A secondary loop that sends tasks to a specific topic using a fixed partition (`PartitionId(0)`) to simulate a work queue, as implemented in `Main.kt:111-126`."},"4283":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher-42","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher#stats","content":"An internal monitoring mechanism that tracks sent messages, partition distribution, last offsets, and task counts via the `Stats` class in `Main.kt:131-165`."},"4284":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher-43","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher#embeddedserver","content":"The Ktor-based HTTP interface providing health checks and real-time statistics via JSON, which is started within the `runBlocking` block in `Main.kt:58-64`."},"4285":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher-44","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher#key-files","content":"File"},"4286":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher-45","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher#key-files","content":"Lines"},"4287":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher-46","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher#key-files","content":"What is there"},"4288":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher-47","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher#key-files","content":"`…/publisher/build.gradle.kts`"},"4289":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher-48","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher#key-files","content":"`1-7`"},"4290":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher-49","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher#key-files","content":"Dependencies for the Ktor server, Booblik client, and serialization."},"4291":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher-50","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher#key-files","content":"`…/publisher/Main.kt`"},"4292":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher-51","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher#key-files","content":"`1-66`"},"4293":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher-52","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher#key-files","content":"The `main` function that orchestrates the connection, producer, and server."},"4294":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher-53","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher#key-files","content":"`…/publisher/Main.kt`"},"4295":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher-54","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher#key-files","content":"`177-200`"},"4296":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher-55","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher#key-files","content":"The `PublisherConfig` data class and its environment-based factory."},"4297":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher-56","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"The `topic.partitionFor(key)` call in `publishForever` is used to determine the partition before sending, because the keyed partitioner is a pure function of the key, whereas a round-robin partitioner would consume a slot and cause records to skip partitions (`Main.kt:97-101`)."},"4298":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher-57","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"The `publishTasks` function explicitly uses `PartitionId(0)` to ensure all tasks go into a single partition to allow any worker to take any task, rather than splitting them by partition (`Main.kt:117-121`)."},"4299":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","type":"page","content":"openConnection","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/publisher"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection"},"4300":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"What this module is responsible for"},"4301":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"4302":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection#the-openconnection-retry-loop","content":"The `openConnection` retry loop"},"4303":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection#broker-availability-and-startup-race-conditions","content":"Broker availability and startup race conditions"},"4304":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection#booblikconnection-instantiation","content":"BooblikConnection instantiation"},"4305":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection#key-files","content":"Key files"},"4306":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"4307":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"4308":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"The `dev/publisher` module is responsible for simulating event streams and task queues to test the Booblik broker. Within this module, the `openConnection` mechanism ensures that the publisher can gracefully handle the lifecycle of the broker, specifically managing the transition from a container starting up to a state where the network socket is actually accepting connections."},"4309":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection#the-openconnection-retry-loop","content":"The `openConnection` function implements a blocking retry mechanism to establish a `BooblikConnection`. It first resolves the broker's location into an `InetSocketAddress` using the host and port provided in the `PublisherConfig` (`Main.kt:77`). If the connection attempt fails, the function catches the exception, prints a retry message including the failure reason, and waits for 1000 milliseconds before attempting to connect again (`Main.kt:82-83`)."},"4310":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection#broker-availability-and-startup-race-conditions","content":"The retry loop is a defensive measure against startup race conditions in containerized environments. While `docker-compose.yml:44-46` uses `condition: service_healthy` to ensure the publisher only starts after the broker's health check passes, the code includes the loop because \"should never\" and \"does not\" are different claims (`Main.kt:72-74`). This prevents the publisher from crashing if the broker's process is running but the network stack is not yet ready to accept connections."},"4311":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection#booblikconnection-instantiation","content":"Once a connection is successfully established, `openConnection` returns a new `BooblikConnection` instance (`Main.kt:80`). Crucially, this connection is initialized with its own `CoroutineScope` configured with a `SupervisorJob` (`Main.kt:80`). This ensures that failures in child coroutines within the connection do not automatically cancel the entire scope, allowing for more robust error handling during the publisher's execution."},"4312":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection#key-files","content":"File"},"4313":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection#key-files","content":"Lines"},"4314":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection#key-files","content":"What is there"},"4315":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection#key-files","content":"`…/publisher/Main.kt`"},"4316":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection#key-files","content":"`76-86`"},"4317":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection#key-files","content":"The `openConnection` implementation containing the retry logic and `InetSocketAddress` setup."},"4318":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection#key-files","content":"`dev/docker-compose.yml`"},"4319":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection#key-files","content":"`44-46`"},"4320":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection#key-files","content":"The `depends_on` configuration using `service_healthy` to coordinate service startup."},"4321":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Scope Isolation**: The `BooblikConnection` created in `openConnection` is given a new `CoroutineScope` with a `SupervisorJob` rather than using the caller's scope, which prevents a connection failure from propagating up and killing the entire publisher application (`Main.kt:80`)."},"4322":{"id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-publisher/openconnection#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**The \"Should Never\" Clause**: Despite the orchestration provided by Docker Compose, the `openConnection` function explicitly includes a `while(true)` loop to handle the edge case where a broker is \"not answering yet\" (`Main.kt:78-82`)."},"4323":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","type":"page","content":"ClaimRecord and the Claims Log","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/queue-worker"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the"},"4324":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"4325":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimrecord","content":"ClaimRecord"},"4326":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimstate","content":"ClaimState"},"4327":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#the-lease-lifecycle","content":"The Lease Lifecycle"},"4328":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#clock-skew-and-determinism","content":"Clock Skew and Determinism"},"4329":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimstatetest","content":"ClaimStateTest"},"4330":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#key-files","content":"Key files"},"4331":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"4332":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"4333":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimrecord","content":"The `ClaimRecord` is the fundamental unit of the claims log, representing either an attempt to take a task or the signal that a task is finished. As defined in `Claims.kt:14-21`, the record contains:"},"4334":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimrecord","content":"Field"},"4335":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimrecord","content":"Type"},"4336":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimrecord","content":"Description"},"4337":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimrecord","content":"`type`"},"4338":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimrecord","content":"`String`"},"4339":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimrecord","content":"Either `claim` or `done`"},"4340":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimrecord","content":"`worker`"},"4341":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimrecord","content":"`String`"},"4342":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimrecord","content":"The identifier of the worker"},"4343":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimrecord","content":"`task`"},"4344":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimrecord","content":"`Long`"},"4345":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimrecord","content":"The unique ID of the task"},"4346":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimrecord","content":"`at`"},"4347":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimrecord","content":"`Long`"},"4348":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimrecord","content":"The claimant's wall clock timestamp"},"4349":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimrecord","content":"`leaseMillis`"},"4350":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimrecord","content":"`Long`"},"4351":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimrecord","content":"The duration the lease is valid for"},"4352":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimrecord","content":"Serialization is handled via `kotlinx.serialization`, with `encode` and `decode` methods provided in the companion object (`Claims.kt:28-31`)."},"4353":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimstate","content":"`ClaimState` is a pure, immutable data structure that represents the current view of the queue by replaying the log. The core logic resides in the `apply` function (`Claims.kt:62-91`), which takes a `ClaimRecord` and the `nextOffset` to produce a new state."},"4354":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-30","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimstate","content":"The state tracks:"},"4355":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-31","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimstate","content":"`consumedUpTo`: The last processed offset in the log."},"4356":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-32","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimstate","content":"`leases`: A map of tasks to their current `Lease` (`Claims.kt:59`)."},"4357":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-33","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimstate","content":"`done`: A set of completed task IDs (`Claims.kt:60`)."},"4358":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-34","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#the-lease-lifecycle","content":"The lifecycle of a task is managed through the sequence of records in the log. A task becomes \"claimable\" when it is not in the `done` set and no active lease exists (`Claims.kt:101-104`)."},"4359":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-35","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#the-lease-lifecycle","content":"**Claiming**: A worker writes a `CLAIM` record. The worker must wait for the log to \"settle\" by reading its own claim back to ensure it actually won the race (`Main.kt:184-193`)."},"4360":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-36","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#the-lease-lifecycle","content":"**Holding**: A task is held if the worker's name and the timestamp `at` match the current lease in the state (`Claims.kt:95-98`)."},"4361":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-37","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#the-lease-lifecycle","content":"**Releasing**: A worker writes a `DONE` record, which removes the task from active leases and adds it to the `done` set (`Claims.kt:67-73`)."},"4362":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-38","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#clock-skew-and-determinism","content":"A critical design requirement is that the verdict is a pure function of the log, ensuring all readers reach the same conclusion even if their local clocks differ. As noted in `Claims.kt:47-51`, the `Lease` is judged by comparing the timestamp written *into the claim itself* against the `at` value of the claim, rather than using the reader's local `now()`. This ensures that skew only affects *when* a worker tries to claim a task, not *who* wins the claim once it is written to the log."},"4363":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-39","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimstatetest","content":"The `ClaimStateTest` class verifies the correctness of the state machine through several property-based scenarios (`ClaimStateTest.kt:22-107`):"},"4364":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-40","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimstatetest","content":"**Race Conditions**: Verifies that the first claim in the log wins and subsequent claims within the lease period are ignored (`ClaimStateTest.kt:45-50`)."},"4365":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-41","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimstatetest","content":"**Lease Expiry**: Ensures a claim written after a previous lease has lapsed successfully takes over the task (`ClaimStateTest.kt:54-59`)."},"4366":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-42","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimstatetest","content":"**Completion**: Confirms that once a `DONE` record is processed, the task is no longer claimable (`ClaimStateTest.kt:64-69`)."},"4367":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-43","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#claimstatetest","content":"**Forward Compatibility**: Asserts that unknown record types only advance the `consumedUpTo` offset without disturbing existing leases (`ClaimStateTest.kt:100-107`)."},"4368":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-44","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#key-files","content":"File"},"4369":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-45","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#key-files","content":"Lines"},"4370":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-46","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#key-files","content":"What is there"},"4371":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-47","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Claims.kt`"},"4372":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-48","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#key-files","content":"`14-43`"},"4373":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-49","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#key-files","content":"Definition of `ClaimRecord` and `Lease` data classes and their serialization logic."},"4374":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-50","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Claims.kt`"},"4375":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-51","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#key-files","content":"`57-91`"},"4376":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-52","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#key-files","content":"The `ClaimState` class containing the `apply` logic for replaying the log."},"4377":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-53","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Main.kt`"},"4378":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-54","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#key-files","content":"`151-217`"},"4379":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-55","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#key-files","content":"The `work` loop in the worker, including the claim-write and settlement-check logic."},"4380":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-56","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#key-files","content":"`…/queue/ClaimStateTest.kt`"},"4381":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-57","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#key-files","content":"`22-107`"},"4382":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-58","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#key-files","content":"Unit tests for the `ClaimState` state machine."},"4383":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-59","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**The Settlement Round Trip**: A worker does not consider itself the owner of a task immediately after sending a claim; it must wait until the claim is read back from the log to confirm it wasn't superseded by a race (`Main.kt:184-193`)."},"4384":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-60","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Clock Independence**: The `holds` function in `ClaimState` (`Claims.kt:95-98`) uses the timestamp stored in the record rather than the current system time, making the state machine deterministic across different machines."},"4385":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the-61","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimrecord-and-the#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**The \"Wasted\" Attempt**: In `Report.kt:82`, an attempt is considered \"lost\" if it was written to the log but did not result in a successful lease, representing work that happened because a worker was \"in step\" with others."},"4386":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","type":"page","content":"ClaimState and the Deterministic Verdict","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/queue-worker"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the"},"4387":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"4388":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimrecord-and-the-log-format","content":"ClaimRecord and the Log Format"},"4389":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimstate-the-pure-function-of-the-log","content":"ClaimState: The Pure Function of the Log"},"4390":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#lease-expiry-and-the-timestamp-based-verdict","content":"Lease Expiry and the Timestamp-based Verdict"},"4391":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#the-claimable-mechanism-and-local-clock-skew","content":"The claimable Mechanism and Local Clock Skew"},"4392":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#verification-of-determinism-in-claimstatetest","content":"Verification of Determinism in ClaimStateTest"},"4393":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"Key files"},"4394":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"Behaviour that surprise"},"4395":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"4396":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","content":"Documentation for the deterministic state machine used in the queue worker module."},"4397":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimrecord-and-the-log-format","content":"The state of the queue is driven by a sequence of `ClaimRecord` entries written to a specific partition of the `claims` topic. As defined in `Claims.kt:14-21`, each record contains the worker's identity, the task ID, and a timestamp. The log format distinguishes between two primary types:"},"4398":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimrecord-and-the-log-format","content":"Type"},"4399":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimrecord-and-the-log-format","content":"Constant"},"4400":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimrecord-and-the-log-format","content":"Description"},"4401":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimrecord-and-the-log-format","content":"`claim`"},"4402":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimrecord-and-the-log-format","content":"`ClaimRecord.CLAIM`"},"4403":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimrecord-and-the-log-format","content":"An attempt by a worker to acquire a lease on a task."},"4404":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimrecord-and-the-log-format","content":"`done`"},"4405":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimrecord-and-the-log-format","content":"`ClaimRecord.DONE`"},"4406":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimrecord-and-the-log-format","content":"A notification that a task has been successfully completed."},"4407":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#claimstate-the-pure-function-of-the-log","content":"The `ClaimState` class acts as a deterministic state machine. The \"verdict\" of the queue is a pure function of the log: by replaying all `ClaimRecord` entries in order using the `apply` function (`Claims.kt:62-92`), any worker can reconstruct the current state of all leases and completed tasks. Because `apply` only relies on the data within the records and not the reader's system clock, the state is perfectly reproducible across different machines."},"4408":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#lease-expiry-and-the-timestamp-based-verdict","content":"A critical design choice is how leases expire. Instead of using the reader's current time, the expiration is judged by the timestamp `at` written into the claim itself. As explained in `Claims.kt:48-51`, a claim is considered \"taken\" if the task is already in the `done` set or if the new claim's timestamp falls within the duration of an existing lease (`Claims.kt:77`). This ensures that if Worker A claims a task at $T=1000$ for 30s, Worker B's claim at $T=1031$ will win, even if Worker B's local clock thinks it is only $T=1010$."},"4409":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#the-claimable-mechanism-and-local-clock-skew","content":"While the final verdict is deterministic, workers still need to decide which tasks they *should* attempt to claim. This is handled by the `claimable` function (`Claims.kt:101-104`), which takes a `now: Long` parameter representing the reader's local clock. This function filters tasks that are not yet `done` and whose current lease has not yet lapsed according to the local time. This is the only place where clock skew matters: it affects *when* a worker decides to try for a task, but it never changes the outcome of who actually wins the task once the claim is written to the log."},"4410":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#verification-of-determinism-in-claimstatetest","content":"The correctness of this logic is verified in `ClaimStateTest.kt`. The tests ensure that:"},"4411":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#verification-of-determinism-in-claimstatetest","content":"The first claim in a sequence wins if subsequent claims land within the lease period (`ClaimStateTest.kt:45-50`)."},"4412":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#verification-of-determinism-in-claimstatetest","content":"A claim arriving after a lease has lapsed successfully takes over the task (`ClaimStateTest.kt:53-60`)."},"4413":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#verification-of-determinism-in-claimstatetest","content":"Once a task is marked as `DONE`, it cannot be claimed again (`ClaimStateTest.kt:63-69`)."},"4414":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#verification-of-determinism-in-claimstatetest","content":"Unknown record types do not disturb existing leases but do advance the `consumedUpTo` offset (`ClaimStateTest.kt:100-107`)."},"4415":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"File"},"4416":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"Lines"},"4417":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-30","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"What is there"},"4418":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-31","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Claims.kt`"},"4419":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-32","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"`14-43`"},"4420":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-33","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"Definition of `ClaimRecord` and its serialization."},"4421":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-34","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Claims.kt`"},"4422":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-35","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"`57-103`"},"4423":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-36","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"The `ClaimState` state machine and lease logic."},"4424":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-37","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"`…/queue/ClaimStateTest.kt`"},"4425":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-38","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"`44-107`"},"4426":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-39","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#key-files","content":"Unit tests for the deterministic state machine."},"4427":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-40","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"**`ClaimState.apply`** is a pure function that ignores the reader's clock; it only cares about the timestamps embedded in the `ClaimRecord` objects."},"4428":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-41","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"**`claimable`** is the only function where the local clock is allowed to influence the logic, and it only affects the *attempt* to claim, not the *result* of the claim."},"4429":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the-42","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/claimstate-and-the#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"**`ClaimRecord.decode`** uses `runCatching` to return `null` for unknown types, allowing the state machine to skip unknown records without crashing or losing its position in the log."},"4430":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","type":"page","content":"dev/queue-worker","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/queue-worker"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker"},"4431":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"What this module is responsible for"},"4432":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"4433":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#claimrecord-and-the-claims-log","content":"ClaimRecord and the Claims Log"},"4434":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#claimstate-and-the-deterministic-verdict","content":"ClaimState and the Deterministic Verdict"},"4435":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-lifecycle-and-the-work-loop","content":"Worker Lifecycle and the Work Loop"},"4436":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"Worker Metrics and Health Monitoring"},"4437":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#queue-report-and-observability","content":"Queue Report and Observability"},"4438":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"Key files"},"4439":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"4440":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 5. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"4441":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"The `dev/queue-worker` module implements a distributed task queue protocol built on top of a log-based broker. Instead of relying on a centralized coordinator with complex locking mechanisms, this module uses a single partition of a `claims` topic as a deterministic arbiter. Workers compete for tasks by writing claims to the log; the order of these records in the log determines the winner."},"4442":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#claimrecord-and-the-claims-log","content":"The coordination relies on a sequence of `ClaimRecord` entries written to a specific topic. These records define the lifecycle of a task through two primary types: `CLAIM` and `DONE` (`Claims.kt:24-25`). Each record contains the worker's identity, the task ID, and a timestamp (`at`) representing the worker's wall clock at the time of the claim (`Claims.kt:18-19`)."},"4443":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#claimrecord-and-the-claims-log","content":"More: ClaimRecord and the Claims Log"},"4444":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#claimstate-and-the-deterministic-verdict","content":"The core logic of the queue is encapsulated in `ClaimState`, which acts as a pure function that folds over the log to produce a consistent view of task ownership (`Claims.kt:57`). A key design feature is that leases are judged using the timestamp written into the `ClaimRecord` itself, rather than the reader's local clock (`Claims.kt:50-51`). This ensures that all workers replaying the same log reach the exact same verdict regarding which worker holds a lease (`Claims.kt:76-85`)."},"4445":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#claimstate-and-the-deterministic-verdict","content":"More: ClaimState and the Deterministic Verdict"},"4446":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-lifecycle-and-the-work-loop","content":"The `work` function manages the continuous loop of task acquisition and execution (`Main.kt:151-217`). The lifecycle follows these steps:"},"4447":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-lifecycle-and-the-work-loop","content":"**Identify**: Find `claimable` tasks by checking the current `ClaimState` (`Main.kt:171`)."},"4448":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-lifecycle-and-the-work-loop","content":"**Claim**: Write a `CLAIM` record to the `claimsTopic` (`Main.kt:178`)."},"4449":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-lifecycle-and-the-work-loop","content":"**Settle**: Wait for the claim to appear in the log by observing `consumedUpTo` (`Main.kt:192`)."},"4450":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-lifecycle-and-the-work-loop","content":"**Execute**: Perform the work for the duration of `workMillis` (`Main.kt:207`)."},"4451":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-lifecycle-and-the-work-loop","content":"**Complete**: Write a `DONE` record to the log (`Main.kt:210`)."},"4452":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-lifecycle-and-the-work-loop","content":"More: Worker Lifecycle and the Work Loop"},"4453":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"Each worker exposes an HTTP server via Ktor to provide observability into its internal state (`Main.kt:68`). The available endpoints are:"},"4454":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"Endpoint"},"4455":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"Method"},"4456":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"Description"},"4457":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"`/health`"},"4458":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"GET"},"4459":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"Returns \"ok\""},"4460":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"`/stats`"},"4461":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-30","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"GET"},"4462":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-31","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"Returns `WorkerStats` including claim latency and task counts"},"4463":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-32","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"`/task/{offset}`"},"4464":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-33","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"GET"},"4465":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-34","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#worker-metrics-and-health-monitoring","content":"Returns `TaskState` for a specific task offset (`Main.kt:77`)"},"4466":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-35","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#queue-report-and-observability","content":"The `Report` object is a standalone tool used to audit the entire queue by replaying the `claims` and `tasks` topics from the beginning (`Report.kt:22`). It calculates the \"wasted\" attempts—claims that lost the race—to measure queue efficiency (`Report.kt:82`). It also performs a critical safety check: if the number of `DONE` records does not match the number of distinct tasks completed, it flags an error indicating a task was worked twice (`Report.kt:98`)."},"4467":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-36","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"File"},"4468":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-37","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"Lines"},"4469":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-38","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"What is there"},"4470":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-39","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`…/queue-worker/build.gradle.kts`"},"4471":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-40","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`1-7`"},"4472":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-41","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"Dependencies for Ktor, serialization, and logback"},"4473":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-42","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Claims.kt`"},"4474":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-43","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`13-33`"},"4475":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-44","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`ClaimRecord` data class and JSON serialization logic"},"4476":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-45","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Claims.kt`"},"4477":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-46","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`57-101`"},"4478":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-47","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`ClaimState` logic for replaying the log and determining leases"},"4479":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-48","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Main.kt`"},"4480":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-49","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`307-341`"},"4481":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-50","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`WorkerConfig` and environment variable mapping"},"4482":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-51","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Report.kt`"},"4483":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-52","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"`22-65`"},"4484":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-53","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#key-files","content":"Logic for replaying topics to generate a queue report"},"4485":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-54","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"The `claimable` function uses the local clock (`now`) to determine if a lease has lapsed, but the actual ownership verdict is determined by the timestamps stored within the `ClaimRecord` itself (`Claims.kt:101`)."},"4486":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-55","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"The `apply` function in `ClaimState` is designed to be a pure function, ensuring that the \"verdict\" is a deterministic result of the log order, regardless of when a worker reads it (`Claims.kt:63`)."},"4487":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker-56","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"In `Main.kt`, a worker must wait for its own claim to \"settle\" (appear in the log) before it begins work, meaning the worker's view of the world is always slightly behind its own actions (`Main.kt:185`)."},"4488":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","type":"page","content":"Worker Lifecycle and the Work Loop","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/queue-worker"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and"},"4489":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#table-of-contents","content":"Table of Contents"},"4490":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"4491":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#the-work-loop-and-task-acquisition","content":"The `work` loop and task acquisition"},"4492":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#the-claimstate-arbiter","content":"The `ClaimState` arbiter"},"4493":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#lease-expiry-and-the-heldat-mechanism","content":"Lease expiry and the `heldAt` mechanism"},"4494":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#redistribution-and-the-check-redistributionsh-scenario","content":"Redistribution and the `check-redistribution.sh` scenario"},"4495":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#workerstats-and-claim-latency","content":"`WorkerStats` and claim latency"},"4496":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"Key files"},"4497":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"4498":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"4499":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#table-of-contents","content":"What this module is responsible for"},"4500":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#table-of-contents","content":"The `work` loop and task acquisition"},"4501":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#table-of-contents","content":"The `ClaimState` arbiter"},"4502":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#table-of-contents","content":"Lease expiry and the `heldAt` mechanism"},"4503":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#table-of-contents","content":"Redistribution and the `check-redistribution.sh` scenario"},"4504":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#table-of-contents","content":"WorkerStats and claim latency"},"4505":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#table-of-contents","content":"Key files"},"4506":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#table-of-contents","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"4507":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#the-work-loop-and-task-acquisition","content":"The main execution loop in `work` continuously attempts to acquire tasks by selecting a candidate from the `claimable` list (`Main.kt:171-172`). Taking a task is not an instantaneous operation; it requires a full round trip where the worker writes a claim and then waits for that claim to appear in its own view of the log (`Main.kt:187-193`). To mitigate collisions when many workers are idle, the `pickRandom` configuration allows workers to pick a random task from the claimable list instead of always picking the first one (`Main.kt:172`)."},"4508":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#the-claimstate-arbiter","content":"The `ClaimState` class acts as the arbiter of the queue by replaying the claims log to determine the current state of all tasks (`Claims.kt:46`). The verdict of who owns a task is a pure function of the log, implemented via the `apply` function, which updates the state based on `ClaimRecord` types (`Claims.kt:63-91`). This ensures that all workers reading the same log prefix reach the same conclusion regarding task ownership and completion."},"4509":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#lease-expiry-and-the-heldat-mechanism","content":"Leases are managed by comparing the timestamp written into a claim against the timestamp of subsequent claims. A lease is considered active if the current claim's timestamp is less than the lease's expiry time, calculated via `heldAt` (`Claims.kt:41`). Crucially, the expiry is judged by the timestamp recorded in the claim itself, not the reader's local clock, which prevents disagreement between workers with clock skew (`Claims.kt:52-54`)."},"4510":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#redistribution-and-the-check-redistributionsh-scenario","content":"The system handles worker failure through lease expiration. If a worker is killed via `SIGKILL` while holding a task, the task is not explicitly released; instead, it becomes `claimable` again once the lease expires in the log (`check-redistribution.sh:54-58`). The `check-redistribution.sh` script verifies that a surviving worker can successfully take over a task previously held by a \"victim\" worker (`check-redistribution.sh:58-62`)."},"4511":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#workerstats-and-claim-latency","content":"Monitoring is provided through `WorkerStats`, which tracks the number of attempts, wins, and losses (`Main.kt:275-288`). The `claimLatencyMicros` measures the time taken for the round trip between writing a claim and seeing it settled in the log (`Main.kt:198`). High collision rates are reflected in the ratio of `won` vs `lost` attempts, where \"lost\" attempts represent work that happened only because no one was there to claim the task (`Report.kt:82-83`)."},"4512":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"File"},"4513":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"Lines"},"4514":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"What is there"},"4515":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Main.kt`"},"4516":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`56-98`"},"4517":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"The `main` function and server setup"},"4518":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Main.kt`"},"4519":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-30","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`151-217`"},"4520":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-31","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"The `work` loop implementation"},"4521":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-32","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Claims.kt`"},"4522":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-33","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`13-40`"},"4523":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-34","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`ClaimRecord` data class and serialization"},"4524":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-35","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Claims.kt`"},"4525":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-36","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`57-91`"},"4526":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-37","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`ClaimState` logic and `apply` function"},"4527":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-38","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Claims.kt`"},"4528":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-39","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`36-40`"},"4529":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-40","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`Lease` data class"},"4530":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-41","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Main.kt`"},"4531":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-42","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`219-272`"},"4532":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-43","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`Stats` class and `snapshot` method"},"4533":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-44","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`…/queue/Main.kt`"},"4534":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-45","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`307-341`"},"4535":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-46","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#key-files","content":"`WorkerConfig` and environment loading"},"4536":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-47","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"The `ClaimState.apply` function is a pure function that never reads a local clock, ensuring that the verdict is a deterministic result of the log content (`Claims.kt:63-91`)."},"4537":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-48","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"A worker's `claimable` tasks are determined by the `now` parameter passed into the function, which allows the worker to use its local clock to decide which tasks to *try* for, even though the *win* is decided by the log's timestamps (`Claims.kt:102-104`)."},"4538":{"id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and-49","page_id":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-queue-worker/worker-lifecycle-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"The `Report` object calculates \"wasted\" attempts by subtracting `wins` from total `attempts`, which is a metric of how many claims were made for tasks already held or finished (`Report.kt:82-83`)."},"4539":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","type":"page","content":"booblikToKafka","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/relay"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka"},"4540":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"What this module is responsible for"},"4541":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"4542":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#the-boobliktokafka-lifecycle","content":"The `booblikToKafka` lifecycle"},"4543":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#the-fileoffsetstore-mechanism","content":"The `FileOffsetStore` mechanism"},"4544":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#at-least-once-delivery-and-kafkaflush","content":"At-least-once delivery and `kafka.flush`"},"4545":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#the-mirrored-topic-and-partition-mapping","content":"The `mirrored` topic and partition mapping"},"4546":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#key-files","content":"Key files"},"4547":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"4548":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"4549":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"The `booblikToKafka` relay acts as a bridge that consumes data from a booblik topic and produces it into a Kafka topic. Unlike the reverse direction, this relay is responsible for managing its own state because the booblik broker does not track consumer positions."},"4550":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#the-boobliktokafka-lifecycle","content":"The lifecycle begins with the `BooblikSubscriber.follow` method, which initiates a stream from the booblik topic using a position retrieved from the `FileOffsetStore` (`Directions.kt:110-111`). The stream is processed via the `collect` operator, which receives batches of records. The `checkpointing` operator is applied to the flow, ensuring that the `OffsetStore` is updated only after the collector has successfully processed the batch (`Directions.kt:112`)."},"4551":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#the-fileoffsetstore-mechanism","content":"Since the booblik broker does not remember readers, the relay maintains state using a `FileOffsetStore` on its own volume (`Directions.kt:93`). To ensure position integrity, the `save` method writes the offset to a temporary file and then performs an atomic move using `StandardCopyOption.ATOMIC_MOVE` (`FileOffsetStore.kt:57`). This prevents a partially written or truncated offset from being parsed during a restart (`FileOffsetStore.kt:25`)."},"4552":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#at-least-once-delivery-and-kafkaflush","content":"The relay guarantees at-least-once delivery through a specific sequence of operations within the `collect` block. First, all records in a batch are sent to Kafka via `kafka.send` (`Directions.kt:117`). Second, `kafka.flush()` is called to ensure all buffered records are actually acknowledged by the Kafka broker (`Directions.kt:119`). Only after the flush is successful does the `checkpointing` mechanism save the `batch.nextOffset` to the `FileOffsetStore` (`Directions.kt:120`)."},"4553":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#the-mirrored-topic-and-partition-mapping","content":"When moving data from booblik to Kafka, the relay faces a limitation: booblik's wire protocol does not store keys, only the `partitionId` (`README.md:177-179`). Consequently, when the relay calls `kafka.send(ProducerRecord(config.kafkaTopic, record))`, it does not provide a key (`Directions.kt:117`). This means the distribution of records into Kafka partitions is determined by Kafka's default partitioning logic rather than any specific key-based rule chosen by the original producer."},"4554":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#key-files","content":"File"},"4555":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#key-files","content":"Lines"},"4556":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#key-files","content":"What is there"},"4557":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#key-files","content":"`…/relay/Directions.kt`"},"4558":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#key-files","content":"`89-124`"},"4559":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#key-files","content":"The `booblikToKafka` function implementation and its logic."},"4560":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#key-files","content":"`…/common/FileOffsetStore.kt`"},"4561":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#key-files","content":"`27-61`"},"4562":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#key-files","content":"The `FileOffsetStore` class and its atomic file-based persistence logic."},"4563":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#key-files","content":"`…/relay/Main.kt`"},"4564":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#key-files","content":"`47-70`"},"4565":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#key-files","content":"The `main` function containing the reconnection loop and error handling."},"4566":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**At-least-once duplicates**: Because the `checkpointing` happens after `kafka.flush()`, a crash between these two calls will result in the same batch being re-sent to Kafka upon restart (`Directions.kt:119-120`)."},"4567":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Key loss**: While the `TopicHandle.send` in the `kafkaToBooblik` direction uses the Kafka key to pick a booblik partition, the `booblikToKafka` direction loses this key entirely because it is not part of the booblik wire protocol (`README.md:177-180`)."},"4568":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/boobliktokafka#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Silent corruption prevention**: The `FileOffsetStore` uses a `.tmp` file and an atomic move specifically to avoid the \"half-saved\" offset bug where a truncated file could lead to silent data replay (`FileOffsetStore.kt:24-25`)."},"4569":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","type":"page","content":"Directional modes","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/relay"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes"},"4570":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"4571":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#directional-configuration-and-environment","content":"Directional Configuration and Environment"},"4572":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#kafka-to-booblik-consumer-group-and-manual-commit","content":"Kafka to booblik: Consumer Group and Manual Commit"},"4573":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#booblik-to-kafka-fileoffsetstore-and-checkpointing","content":"booblik to Kafka: FileOffsetStore and Checkpointing"},"4574":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#the-key-to-partition-mapping","content":"The Key-to-Partition Mapping"},"4575":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#key-files","content":"Key files"},"4576":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"4577":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"4578":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#directional-configuration-and-environment","content":"The behavior of the relay is determined by the `Direction` enum, which is parsed from the `RELAY_DIRECTION` environment variable (defaulting to `KAFKA_TO_BOOBLIK`) in `Main.kt:159-163`. The `RelayConfig` class encapsulates all necessary parameters, including bootstrap servers and topic names, which are loaded from the environment in `Main.kt:157-174`."},"4579":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#directional-configuration-and-environment","content":"Environment Variable"},"4580":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#directional-configuration-and-environment","content":"`RelayConfig` Property"},"4581":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#directional-configuration-and-environment","content":"Default Value"},"4582":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#directional-configuration-and-environment","content":"`RELAY_DIRECTION`"},"4583":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#directional-configuration-and-environment","content":"`direction`"},"4584":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#directional-configuration-and-environment","content":"`KAFKA_TO_BOOBLIK`"},"4585":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#directional-configuration-and-environment","content":"`BOOBLIK_HOST`"},"4586":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#directional-configuration-and-environment","content":"`brokerHost`"},"4587":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#directional-configuration-and-environment","content":"`127.0.0.1`"},"4588":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#directional-configuration-and-environment","content":"`KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP`"},"4589":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#directional-configuration-and-environment","content":"`kafkaBootstrap`"},"4590":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#directional-configuration-and-environment","content":"`kafka:9092`"},"4591":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#directional-configuration-and-environment","content":"`KAFKA_TOPIC`"},"4592":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#directional-configuration-and-environment","content":"`kafkaTopic`"},"4593":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#directional-configuration-and-environment","content":"`orders`"},"4594":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#kafka-to-booblik-consumer-group-and-manual-commit","content":"When operating in the `KAFKA_TO_BOOBLIK` direction, the relay acts as a Kafka consumer. To ensure delivery guarantees, `ENABLE_AUTO_COMMIT_CONFIG` is explicitly set to `false` in `Directions.kt:49`. The process follows a strict lifecycle: it polls records, sends them to booblik via a `Producer`, and only after the booblik acknowledgements are received does it call `consumer.commitSync()` in `Directions.kt:71`. This ensures that the Kafka consumer group position only advances once the data is safely in booblik."},"4595":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#booblik-to-kafka-fileoffsetstore-and-checkpointing","content":"In the `BOOBLIK_TO_KAFKA` direction, the broker does not track the reader's position. Instead, the relay uses a `FileOffsetStore` to persist the state locally on a volume, as seen in `Directions.kt:93`. The `booblikToKafka` function uses a `checkpointing` operator that saves the offset only after the `collector` has successfully handled the batch, ensuring that the `FileOffsetStore` is updated only after the Kafka producer has finished its work (`Directions.kt:112`)."},"4596":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#the-key-to-partition-mapping","content":"The relay handles Kafka keys differently depending on the direction. In `kafkaToBooblik`, the Kafka record key is passed to `handle.send(record.value(), key = record.key())` in `Directions.kt:66`, which allows the key to influence partition selection in booblik, preserving per-key ordering. However, because the booblik wire format does not contain a field for the key, the key itself is not stored and is lost for any subsequent round trip (`Main.kt:37-42`)."},"4597":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#key-files","content":"File"},"4598":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#key-files","content":"Lines"},"4599":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#key-files","content":"What is there"},"4600":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-30","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#key-files","content":"`…/relay/Main.kt`"},"4601":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-31","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#key-files","content":"`83-86`"},"4602":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-32","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#key-files","content":"`Direction` enum definition"},"4603":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-33","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#key-files","content":"`…/relay/Main.kt`"},"4604":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-34","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#key-files","content":"`142-153`"},"4605":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-35","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#key-files","content":"`RelayConfig` data class and environment loading"},"4606":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-36","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#key-files","content":"`…/relay/Directions.kt`"},"4607":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-37","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#key-files","content":"`38-77`"},"4608":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-38","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#key-files","content":"`kafkaToBooblik` implementation"},"4609":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-39","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#key-files","content":"`…/relay/Directions.kt`"},"4610":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-40","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#key-files","content":"`89-124`"},"4611":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-41","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#key-files","content":"`booblikToKafka` implementation"},"4612":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-42","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"The `kafkaToBooblik` function uses `withContext(Dispatchers.IO)` to wrap the blocking `consumer.poll` and `consumer.commitSync` calls to avoid blocking the coroutine scope (`Directions.kt:62, 71`)."},"4613":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-43","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"In the `BOOBLIK_TO_KAFKA` direction, the relay uses `kafka.flush()` before calling `stats.observe` to ensure that all buffered records are actually sent to Kafka before the position is updated (`Directions.kt:119`)."},"4614":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes-44","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev-relay/directional-modes#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"The `RelayConfig` uses `replace` logic for environment variables, where the `direction` string is transformed by converting it to uppercase and replacing hyphens with underscores (`Main.kt:160-162`)."},"4615":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay","type":"page","content":"dev/relay","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev/relay"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-relay"},"4616":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-relay#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"4617":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev-relay#directional-modes","content":"Directional modes"},"4618":{"id":"/wiki/dev-relay-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev-relay","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wi