57":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-9","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"4758":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-10","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-main-and-quick-configurations","content":"The module provides different levels of rigor to balance the need for valid data against the need for rapid feedback. The `main` configuration is the only one suitable for recording official numbers, utilizing 5 warmups and 10 iterations (`build.gradle.kts:65-67`), whereas the `quick` configuration is intended only for order-of-magnitude checks and is not suitable for the official record due to its wide confidence intervals (`build.gradle.kts:122-124`)."},"4759":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-11","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-ci-configuration-and-the-disk-barrier","content":"To prevent noise caused by the I/O variance of hosted runners, the `ci` configuration explicitly excludes `GroupCommitBenchmark` and the `flushEveryAppend` parameter (`build.gradle.kts:115-116`). Instead of checking for percentage-based regressions which are unreliable on shared hardware, the CI workflow uses `benchmark-floor.sh` to detect only \"collapses\" where performance drops by an order of magnitude (`benchmark.yml:75`)."},"4760":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-12","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-runtimefootprint-verification","content":"To prevent silent invalidation of results where benchmarks run under a different JVM profile than the production broker, the `RuntimeFootprint` object verifies that required flags like `-Xmx64M` and `-XX:+UseSerialGC` are present (`RuntimeFootprint.kt:24-30`). This ensures that the measured process matches the intended deployment footprint."},"4761":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-13","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-measurementdir-and-filesystem-constraints","content":"The benchmark refuses to run on volatile filesystems like `tmpfs` (which is used for `/tmp` on Ubuntu 26.04) because `fsync` behavior on such systems is non-functional (`build.gradle.kts:32-34`). Instead, it forces all measurements to be written to an absolute path in the `build/measurements` directory on the physical disk (`build.gradle.kts:35-39`)."},"4762":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-14","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-allopen-plugin-and-state-classes","content":"Because JMH generates a subclass of every `@State` class, Kotlin's default `final` class modifier would cause generation to fail with a compiler-like error message (`build.gradle.kts:46-50`). The `allOpen` plugin is used to ensure these classes are non-final for the JMH generator."},"4763":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-15","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-probe-task-lifecycle","content":"Beyond JMH, the module defines several `probe` tasks which are ordinary `JavaExec` programs designed to measure specific behavioral \"shapes\" rather than averages (`build.gradle.kts:132-140`). These include:"},"4764":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-16","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-probe-task-lifecycle","content":"Task"},"4765":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-17","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-probe-task-lifecycle","content":"Description"},"4766":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-18","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-probe-task-lifecycle","content":"`probeDurability`"},"4767":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-19","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-probe-task-lifecycle","content":"M-24: does `msync` promise what `fsync` promises"},"4768":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-20","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-probe-task-lifecycle","content":"`probeStartup`"},"4769":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-21","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-probe-task-lifecycle","content":"M-23: how fast recovery scans a log"},"4770":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-22","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-probe-task-lifecycle","content":"`probeSustainedWrite`"},"4771":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-23","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-probe-task-lifecycle","content":"M-26: throughput once the log outgrows memory"},"4772":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-24","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-probe-task-lifecycle","content":"`probeLoad`"},"4773":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-25","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-probe-task-lifecycle","content":"M-33/M-34: end-to-end RPS and latency percentiles over a socket"},"4774":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-26","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-probe-task-lifecycle","content":"`probeRemoteLoad`"},"4775":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-27","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#the-probe-task-lifecycle","content":"M-38: the same load against a broker on another machine"},"4776":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-28","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"File"},"4777":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-29","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"Lines"},"4778":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-30","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"What is there"},"4779":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-31","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"`booblik-benchmark/build.gradle.kts`"},"4780":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-32","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"`57-86`"},"4781":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-33","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"`benchmark` configuration block and target registration"},"4782":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-34","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"`booblik-benchmark/build.gradle.kts`"},"4783":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-35","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"`132-154`"},"4784":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-36","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"`probes` map and task registration"},"4785":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-37","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"`…/benchmark/RuntimeFootprint.kt`"},"4786":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-38","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"`24-46`"},"4787":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-39","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#key-files","content":"JVM argument verification logic"},"4788":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-40","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**`msync` vs `fsync`**: In certain environments, `msync` can appear significantly faster than `fsync` because it may not provide the same durability guarantees, making it an invalid comparison for durability (`benchmarking.md:157-162`)."},"4789":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-41","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**`MAPPED` performance**: While `MAPPED` mode is significantly faster for small writes, its performance can be heavily impacted by segment rolling (re-mapping) during long iterations (`benchmarking.md:130-135`)."},"4790":{"id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and-42","page_id":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/benchmark-execution-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**`SELECTOR` vs `VIRTUAL_THREADS`**: In the networking layer, `SELECTOR` provides significantly lower latency and higher throughput than `VIRTUAL_THREADS` because `transferTo` blocks on disk I/O, which pins the virtual thread (`benchmarking.md:396-402`)."},"4791":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","type":"page","content":"Docker image lifecycle and GHCR publishing","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki",".github"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle"},"4792":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-0","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"4793":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-1","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#the-release-workflow-and-tag-based-publishing","content":"The `release` workflow and tag-based publishing"},"4794":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-2","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#the-check-gate-and-distribution-integrity","content":"The `check` gate and distribution integrity"},"4795":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-3","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#the-docker-smoke-conformance-kit","content":"The `docker-smoke` conformance kit"},"4796":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-4","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#the-sparse-segment-and-volume-persistence","content":"The `sparse` segment and volume persistence"},"4797":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-5","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#the-latest-tag-and-registry-synchronization","content":"The `latest` tag and registry synchronization"},"4798":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-6","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"Key files"},"4799":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-7","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"4800":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-8","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"4801":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-9","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#the-release-workflow-and-tag-based-publishing","content":"The publishing process is triggered by git tags matching the `v*` pattern or via manual dispatch `release.yml:12`. The workflow performs a critical transformation of the repository name into a lowercase registry path to comply with container registry requirements `release.yml:54`. The version is derived by stripping the leading 'v' from the git tag, ensuring that a tag like `v0.1.0` results in an image tagged `:0.1.0` `release.yml:50`."},"4802":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-10","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#the-check-gate-and-distribution-integrity","content":"To prevent the \"shipping of unchecked code,\" the `release` workflow enforces a strict order of operations. It first runs `./gradlew check` as a gate `release.yml:62`. The `ci/docker-build.sh` script is designed to respect this by explicitly running the gate before performing the `:booblik-app:installDist` task `docker-build.sh:21-27`. This ensures the image is built from a distribution that has already passed all tests, rather than building from a potentially broken local workspace `docker-build.sh:5-7`."},"4803":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-11","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#the-docker-smoke-conformance-kit","content":"The `ci/docker-smoke.sh` script performs deep inspection of the running container to ensure the runtime environment matches the measured profile. It validates that the process runs as the `booblik` user rather than `root` `docker-smoke.sh:95`. Crucially, it parses the `jvm:` line in the logs to verify that six specific flags—including `-Xmx64M` and `-XX:MaxDirectMemorySize=32M`—are present `docker-smoke.sh:76-83`. It also verifies that the broker's health check responds to `METADATA` requests `docker-smoke.sh:87`."},"4804":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-12","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#the-sparse-segment-and-volume-persistence","content":"A specific edge case is tested to ensure that the storage engine's performance characteristics are preserved in the image. The smoke test checks that when a segment is created, it remains \"sparse\" on the filesystem `docker-smoke.sh:104`. It does this by inspecting the `stat` output of the segment file to ensure the allocated blocks are significantly lower than the apparent file size `docker-smoke.sh:102-104`."},"4805":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-13","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#the-latest-tag-and-registry-synchronization","content":"Once the image is verified, the workflow performs a dual-push to GHCR. It tags the specific versioned image (e.g., `:0.1.0`) and the `:latest` pointer `release.yml:87-89`. This ensures that users can always pull the most recent stable version via the `:latest` tag while maintaining a permanent record of specific releases `release.yml:88`."},"4806":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-14","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"File"},"4807":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-15","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"Lines"},"4808":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-16","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"What is there"},"4809":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-17","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`…/workflows/release.yml`"},"4810":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-18","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`10-23`"},"4811":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-19","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"Workflow trigger conditions and manual input definitions"},"4812":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-20","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`…/workflows/release.yml`"},"4813":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-21","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`39-58`"},"4814":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-22","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"Version extraction logic and registry path formatting"},"4815":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-23","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`ci/docker-smoke.sh`"},"4816":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-24","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`76-83`"},"4817":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-25","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"JVM runtime profile flag verification"},"4818":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-26","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`ci/docker-build.sh`"},"4819":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-27","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`21-26`"},"4820":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-28","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"Gate execution logic"},"4821":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-29","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`README.md`"},"4822":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-30","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"`69-72`"},"4823":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-31","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#key-files","content":"The specific JVM profile required for measurement"},"4824":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-32","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**The `pipefail` trap**: In `docker-smoke.sh:39`, using `grep` in a pipeline with `set -o pipefail` can cause a successful match to return a failure code (141) because `grep` exits early and sends a `SIGPIPE` to `docker logs`."},"4825":{"id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle-33","page_id":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github/docker-image-lifecycle#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**The `installDist` dependency**: The `ci/docker-build.sh` script does not use a multi-stage Dockerfile; instead, it relies on the host to run `:booblik-app:installDist` first to ensure the image contains the exact distribution that passed the gate `docker-build.sh:27-32`."},"4826":{"id":"/wiki/github","page_id":"/wiki/github","type":"page","content":".github","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki",".github"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github"},"4827":{"id":"/wiki/github-0","page_id":"/wiki/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"What this module is responsible for"},"4828":{"id":"/wiki/github-1","page_id":"/wiki/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"4829":{"id":"/wiki/github-2","page_id":"/wiki/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github#benchmark-execution-and-reporting","content":"Benchmark execution and reporting"},"4830":{"id":"/wiki/github-3","page_id":"/wiki/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github#the-build-gate-and-smoke-testing","content":"The build gate and smoke testing"},"4831":{"id":"/wiki/github-4","page_id":"/wiki/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github#docker-image-lifecycle-and-ghcr-publishing","content":"Docker image lifecycle and GHCR publishing"},"4832":{"id":"/wiki/github-5","page_id":"/wiki/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github#maven-dependency-verification-and-multiplatform-publishing","content":"Maven dependency verification and multiplatform publishing"},"4833":{"id":"/wiki/github-6","page_id":"/wiki/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github#key-files","content":"Key files"},"4834":{"id":"/wiki/github-7","page_id":"/wiki/github","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/github#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"Behaviour that surprise"},"4835":{"id":"/wiki/github-8","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 4. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"4836":{"id":"/wiki/github-9","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"This module contains the CI/CD pipelines and release automation for the `booblik` project, covering benchmarking, code verification, Docker image distribution, and Maven artifact publishing."},"4837":{"id":"/wiki/github-10","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#benchmark-execution-and-reporting","content":"The benchmark suite is designed to catch massive performance collapses rather than subtle regressions. Because hosted runners are inconsistent machines, the workflow `benchmark.yml:10-12` warns that a 20% swing between runs is expected and should be ignored. The job runs `mainCiBenchmark` to avoid disk-bound noise and uses a \"floor\" mechanism to fail only if performance drops by an order of magnitude, as defined in `benchmark.yml:72-75`."},"4838":{"id":"/wiki/github-11","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#benchmark-execution-and-reporting","content":"More: Benchmark execution and reporting"},"4839":{"id":"/wiki/github-12","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#the-build-gate-and-smoke-testing","content":"The `check` job in `build.yml:14` acts as the primary gate, encompassing tests and `ktlint` to ensure local and CI environments remain in sync. This is followed by a smoke test of the packaged broker via `./ci/smoke.sh` as seen in `build.yml:47-48`. Additionally, the `sample` job ensures that the published client can actually be used by running real-world scenarios like partition splitting and task queueing in `build.yml:138-140`."},"4840":{"id":"/wiki/github-13","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#docker-image-lifecycle-and-ghcr-publishing","content":"Docker images are published to GHCR upon receiving a git tag, as specified in `release.yml:10-13`. The process involves building the image for `amd64` and pushing both the specific version tag and the `:latest` tag, as detailed in `release.yml:84-89`. Before pushing, the image undergoes a smoke test via `./ci/docker-smoke.sh` to ensure the container is functional, as seen in `release.yml:71-72`."},"4841":{"id":"/wiki/github-14","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#docker-image-lifecycle-and-ghcr-publishing","content":"More: Docker image lifecycle and GHCR publishing"},"4842":{"id":"/wiki/github-15","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#maven-dependency-verification-and-multiplatform-publishing","content":"Publishing is a manual decision that targets JVM and Native libraries. Because multiplatform modules must be published from a single host to maintain target consistency, the workflow runs on `macos-15` as seen in `publish.yml:39`. The process includes a rigorous verification step that checks POM files to ensure dependencies like `booblik-core` and `kotlinx-coroutines-core-jvm` are correctly exposed at compile scope, preventing unresolvable dependencies for consumers, as described in `publish.yml:114-144`."},"4843":{"id":"/wiki/github-16","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#key-files","content":"File"},"4844":{"id":"/wiki/github-17","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#key-files","content":"Lines"},"4845":{"id":"/wiki/github-18","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#key-files","content":"What is there"},"4846":{"id":"/wiki/github-19","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#key-files","content":"`…/workflows/benchmark.yml`"},"4847":{"id":"/wiki/github-20","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#key-files","content":"`33-36`"},"4848":{"id":"/wiki/github-21","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#key-files","content":"Weekly cron schedule for benchmarks"},"4849":{"id":"/wiki/github-22","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#key-files","content":"`…/workflows/build.yml`"},"4850":{"id":"/wiki/github-23","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#key-files","content":"`14-16`"},"4851":{"id":"/wiki/github-24","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#key-files","content":"The `check` job configuration"},"4852":{"id":"/wiki/github-25","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#key-files","content":"`…/workflows/publish.yml`"},"4853":{"id":"/wiki/github-26","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#key-files","content":"`39`"},"4854":{"id":"/wiki/github-27","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#key-files","content":"The `macos-15` runner requirement for multiplatform"},"4855":{"id":"/wiki/github-28","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#key-files","content":"`…/workflows/release.yml`"},"4856":{"id":"/wiki/github-29","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#key-files","content":"`10-13`"},"4857":{"id":"/wiki/github-30","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#key-files","content":"Triggering via git tags"},"4858":{"id":"/wiki/github-31","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"The `mainCiBenchmark` task is used instead of `mainBenchmark` to avoid noise from disk-bound operations like `GroupCommitBenchmark` and `SegmentAppendBenchmark`, as noted in `benchmark.yml:22-24`."},"4859":{"id":"/wiki/github-32","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"The `sample` job is the only one that exercises the **published** client against the **published** image to catch packaging mistakes that a standard build would miss, as explained in `build.yml:104-109`."},"4860":{"id":"/wiki/github-33","page_id":"/wiki/github","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/github#behaviour-that-surprise","content":"A `publish` action can be triggered from any ref, which is why a `check` step is explicitly included to prevent publishing broken code, as stated in `publish.yml:60-62`."},"4861":{"id":"/wiki/dev","page_id":"/wiki/dev","type":"page","content":"dev","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev"},"4862":{"id":"/wiki/dev-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"What this module is responsible for"},"4863":{"id":"/wiki/dev-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"4864":{"id":"/wiki/dev-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev#partitioning-by-key-and-consumer-side-position","content":"Partitioning by key and consumer-side position"},"4865":{"id":"/wiki/dev-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev#the-claims-log-as-a-coordinator-less-task-queue","content":"The claims log as a coordinator-less task queue"},"4866":{"id":"/wiki/dev-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev#state-as-a-fold-over-the-log","content":"State as a fold over the log"},"4867":{"id":"/wiki/dev-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev#relay-between-kafka-and-booblik","content":"Relay between Kafka and booblik"},"4868":{"id":"/wiki/dev-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev#build-and-dependency-configuration","content":"Build and dependency configuration"},"4869":{"id":"/wiki/dev-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"Key files"},"4870":{"id":"/wiki/dev-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"4871":{"id":"/wiki/dev-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"4872":{"id":"/wiki/dev-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#what-this-module-is-responsible-for","content":"This module provides a demonstration of distributed systems patterns using the `booblik` broker. It implements four distinct architectural layers: partition-based event streaming, a coordinator-less task queue, stateful projections, and a Kafka relay."},"4873":{"id":"/wiki/dev-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#partitioning-by-key-and-consumer-side-position","content":"In the first layer, the system demonstrates how to achieve ordered processing for specific entities. The key (e.g., a user ID) is used by the client to determine the partition number, ensuring all events for a single user land in the same partition (`README.md:19-22`). Unlike Kafka consumer groups, the assignment here is static and managed via the environment. Crucially, the consumer is responsible for its own position using a `FileOffsetStore`, which persists the offset to a volume via an atomic move to ensure that a crash during checkpointing does not result in corrupted state (`README.md:30-33`)."},"4874":{"id":"/wiki/dev-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#partitioning-by-key-and-consumer-side-position","content":"More: Partitioning by key and consumer-side position"},"4875":{"id":"/wiki/dev-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#the-claims-log-as-a-coordinator-less-task-queue","content":"Layer 2 implements a task queue where workers compete for tasks without a central coordinator. Instead of locks, workers append \"claims\" to a specific partition of a `claims` topic; the first claim to land in the log wins (`README.md:68-70`). A lease is determined by a timestamp written into the claim itself, meaning workers reach the same conclusion about a lapsed lease even if their local clocks are skewed (`README.md:72-75`). This design avoids a central coordinator but introduces specific trade-offs:"},"4876":{"id":"/wiki/dev-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#the-claims-log-as-a-coordinator-less-task-queue","content":"**At-least-once delivery:** A worker might finish a task after its lease has expired and another worker has taken it (`README.md:85-87`)."},"4877":{"id":"/wiki/dev-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#the-claims-log-as-a-coordinator-less-task-queue","content":"**Log growth:** Every task requires two records in the claims log (`README.md:88`)."},"4878":{"id":"/wiki/dev-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#the-claims-log-as-a-coordinator-less-task-queue","content":"**Scaling limits:** Traffic scales as `workers × tasks` because every worker must read every claim (`README.md:91-92`)."},"4879":{"id":"/wiki/dev-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#state-as-a-fold-over-the-log","content":"The projection layer treats state as a pure function of the log. The service does not persist its own state; instead, it rebuilds its view by replaying the log from the beginning (`README.md:129-131`). To ensure no data is lost or duplicated during a restart, the service uses a two-phase approach: `replay()` catches up to the high watermark, and `follow()` continues from that point (`README.md:133-136`). A critical safety feature is that the service does not persist its position separately from the state; persisting a position without the corresponding state would lead to silent corruption (`README.md:141-144`)."},"4880":{"id":"/wiki/dev-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#state-as-a-fold-over-the-log","content":"More: State as a fold over the log"},"4881":{"id":"/wiki/dev-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#relay-between-kafka-and-booblik","content":"Layer 4 provides a bidirectional bridge between Kafka and `booblik`. Because `booblik` does not implement the full Kafka protocol (such as `baseOffset` and CRC), the relay performs translation in user space (`README.md:162-166`). The direction of data flow is determined by the environment configuration. The position management differs by direction:"},"4882":{"id":"/wiki/dev-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#relay-between-kafka-and-booblik","content":"Direction"},"4883":{"id":"/wiki/dev-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#relay-between-kafka-and-booblik","content":"Who remembers the position"},"4884":{"id":"/wiki/dev-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#relay-between-kafka-and-booblik","content":"Kafka $\\to$ booblik"},"4885":{"id":"/wiki/dev-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#relay-between-kafka-and-booblik","content":"Kafka (via consumer group, auto-commit off)"},"4886":{"id":"/wiki/dev-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#relay-between-kafka-and-booblik","content":"booblik $\\to$ Kafka"},"4887":{"id":"/wiki/dev-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#relay-between-kafka-and-booblik","content":"The relay (via `FileOffsetStore` on a volume)"},"4888":{"id":"/wiki/dev-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#relay-between-kafka-and-booblik","content":"(`README.md:174-175`)"},"4889":{"id":"/wiki/dev-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#build-and-dependency-configuration","content":"The module uses Gradle with Kotlin DSL. The `subprojects` block in `build.gradle.kts:7-38` applies the Kotlin JVM and serialization plugins to all subprojects. A significant configuration detail is the avoidance of `repositories { }` blocks within `subprojects`, as doing so would replace rather than append to the repositories defined in `settings.gradle.kts` (`build.gradle.kts:21-24`). Additionally, the broker's runtime profile (e.g., 64 MiB memory constraints) is intentionally not applied to the sample services to ensure the benchmarks reflect the service's actual work rather than the broker's constraints (`build.gradle.kts:31-37`)."},"4890":{"id":"/wiki/dev-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"File"},"4891":{"id":"/wiki/dev-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"Lines"},"4892":{"id":"/wiki/dev-30","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"What is there"},"4893":{"id":"/wiki/dev-31","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`dev/README.md`"},"4894":{"id":"/wiki/dev-32","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`1-224`"},"4895":{"id":"/wiki/dev-33","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"Detailed architectural documentation of the four layers and known defects."},"4896":{"id":"/wiki/dev-34","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`dev/build.gradle.kts`"},"4897":{"id":"/wiki/dev-35","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`1-38`"},"4898":{"id":"/wiki/dev-36","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"Gradle configuration for subprojects and dependency management."},"4899":{"id":"/wiki/dev-37","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`dev/check-projection.sh`"},"4900":{"id":"/wiki/dev-38","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`1-50`"},"4901":{"id":"/wiki/dev-39","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"Script to verify projection replay and state consistency."},"4902":{"id":"/wiki/dev-40","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`dev/check-queue.sh`"},"4903":{"id":"/wiki/dev-41","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`1-20`"},"4904":{"id":"/wiki/dev-42","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"Script to assert task ownership in the queue layer."},"4905":{"id":"/wiki/dev-43","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`dev/check-redistribution.sh`"},"4906":{"id":"/wiki/dev-44","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`1-68`"},"4907":{"id":"/wiki/dev-45","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"Script to test task redistribution after a worker is killed."},"4908":{"id":"/wiki/dev-46","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`dev/check-relay.sh`"},"4909":{"id":"/wiki/dev-47","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"`1-50`"},"4910":{"id":"/wiki/dev-48","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#key-files","content":"Script to verify the Kafka $\\leftrightarrow$ booblik round trip."},"4911":{"id":"/wiki/dev-49","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Silent Replay:** If a `FileOffsetStore` is truncated, it can cause a consumer to silently replay records because the parser handles truncated strings without error (`README.md:32-33`)."},"4912":{"id":"/wiki/dev-50","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**The `partitionFor(null)` Trap:** Calling `partitionFor(null)` advances an internal counter, which can cause records to skip partitions if the user calls the function once to \"check\" and then again to \"send\" (`README.md:204-206`)."},"4913":{"id":"/wiki/dev-51","page_id":"/wiki/dev","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**The `receive` Cancellation Bug:** In version `0.1.2`, cancelling a `mailbox.receive` call could pull an element off a channel and drop it, causing the producer to hang while the element was lost (`README.md:219-222`)."},"4914":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","type":"page","content":"Partitioning by key and consumer-side position","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and"},"4915":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"4916":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#the-keyed-partitioner","content":"The Keyed Partitioner"},"4917":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#fileoffsetstore","content":"FileOffsetStore"},"4918":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#at-least-once-checkpointing","content":"At-least-once Checkpointing"},"4919":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#recovery-after-a-crash","content":"Recovery after a crash"},"4920":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#the-split-assertion","content":"The split assertion"},"4921":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"Key files"},"4922":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"4923":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"4924":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#the-keyed-partitioner","content":"The client uses a key to determine which partition a record belongs to before the record is sent. As described in `README.md:19-21`, the `TopicHandle` hashes the key and sends the broker a partition number; the key itself never reaches the wire. This ensures that all events for a specific user are routed to the same partition, allowing a single service to handle them in order."},"4925":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#fileoffsetstore","content":"To ensure that a consumer's position survives a process restart, the `FileOffsetStore` implements `OffsetStore` by writing the position to a file on a volume. As detailed in `README.md:30-32`, this is achieved through a temporary file and an atomic move to prevent corruption. This mechanism ensures that a \"half-saved\" position is not written, which would otherwise cause silent replays of truncated offsets."},"4926":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#at-least-once-checkpointing","content":"The system guarantees at-least-once delivery by managing the lifecycle of a batch carefully. According to `README.md:35-37`, `checkpointing` saves the offset only **after** the collector has successfully handled the batch. If a failure occurs between the handling of the batch and the saving of the offset, the batch will be replayed upon restart."},"4927":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#recovery-after-a-crash","content":"When a consumer restarts, it uses its persisted position to resume reading. The `resumedFrom` field in the consumer's `/stats` endpoint provides visibility into this process (`README.md:52-53`). As noted in `check.py:14-16`, resuming slightly **behind** the last known position is considered legal behavior because the at-least-once guarantee allows for the replay of a batch that was handled but not yet checkpointed."},"4928":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#the-split-assertion","content":"The integrity of the partitioning and consumption is validated by an external check. The `check.sh` script triggers a validation where `check.py` asserts that every record sent by the publisher reaches exactly one consumer (`README.md:44-47`). The `split` mode in `check.py:25-38` verifies that the number of records written to a partition matches the position held by the consumer assigned to that partition."},"4929":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"File"},"4930":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"Lines"},"4931":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"What is there"},"4932":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`dev/README.md`"},"4933":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`19-22`"},"4934":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"Mechanics of keyed partitioning and partition assignment."},"4935":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`dev/README.md`"},"4936":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`30-33`"},"4937":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"Implementation details of `FileOffsetStore` and atomic moves."},"4938":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`dev/README.md`"},"4939":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`35-38`"},"4940":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"Explanation of at-least-once checkpointing logic."},"4941":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`dev/README.md`"},"4942":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`52-53`"},"4943":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"Behavior of the `resumedFrom` field during restarts."},"4944":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`dev/check.py`"},"4945":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-30","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`25-38`"},"4946":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-31","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"Logic for asserting partition/position matches."},"4947":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-32","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`dev/check.sh`"},"4948":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-33","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"`40-48`"},"4949":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-34","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#key-files","content":"Orchestration of the split assertion check."},"4950":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-35","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Silent Replays**: Because checkpointing happens after processing, a consumer might start from a position slightly behind its last successful work, causing it to replay the last batch (`README.md:36-37`)."},"4951":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-36","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Key Erasure**: A Kafka key does not survive the crossing into booblik; while it is used to pick the partition on the way in, it is not stored in the booblik wire format and cannot be recovered on the way back (`README.md:177-180`)."},"4952":{"id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and-37","page_id":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/partitioning-key-and#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"**Non-monotonicity in `partitionFor(null)`**: Calling `partitionFor(null)` advances an internal counter, meaning that checking which partition a record *would* go to before actually sending it can cause records to skip partitions (`README.md:204-206`)."},"4953":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","type":"page","content":"State as a fold over the log","breadcrumbs":["Docs","Wiki","dev"],"tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over"},"4954":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-0","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#diagram","content":"Diagram"},"4955":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-1","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-lifecycle","content":"The Projection lifecycle"},"4956":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-2","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"The Projection state and Progress"},"4957":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-3","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#rebuilt-view-and-the-replay-guarantee","content":"Rebuilt view and the replay guarantee"},"4958":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-4","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#key-files","content":"Key files"},"4959":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-5","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#behaviour-that-surprises","content":"Behaviour that surprises"},"4960":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-6","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","content":"Model `gemma-mtp`, commit `f508a4b65b3f`, 2026-08-15, sources: 6. Edit the code or the hand-written documentation instead."},"4961":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-7","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-lifecycle","content":"The projection transitions from a historical reconstruction to a live stream through two distinct phases. First, it performs a `replay()` from the `Earliest` position to catch up with history, a process that ends when the stream reaches the high watermark as it was when the service started (`Main.kt:98-104`). Once the replay is complete, the service enters the `follow()` phase, which does not end, allowing the view to stay current (`Main.kt:108-128`). The seamless transition between these two phases is achieved via `RecordBatch.nextOffset`, which ensures the `follow` starts exactly where the `replay` stopped, preventing gaps or double-counting (`Main.kt:36-39`)."},"4962":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-8","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"The projection is designed such that the service stores nothing locally; its state is purely a functional fold over the log (`Main.kt:28-30`). To avoid silent corruption, the service deliberately does not persist its position; persisting a position without the corresponding state would result in a service that resumes at a late offset with an empty view (`Main.kt:40-42`). The current status of the service is exposed via `ProjectionStats`, which includes:"},"4963":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-9","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"Field"},"4964":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-10","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"Description"},"4965":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-11","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"`replayComplete`"},"4966":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-12","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"Whether the historical replay has finished"},"4967":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-13","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"`live`"},"4968":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-14","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"Whether the service is currently following the tail"},"4969":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-15","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"`fromReplay`"},"4970":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-16","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"Total events processed during the replay phase"},"4971":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-17","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"`fromFollow`"},"4972":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-18","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"Total events processed during the follow phase"},"4973":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-19","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"`applied`"},"4974":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-20","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"Total events applied to the projection"},"4975":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-21","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"`users`"},"4976":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-22","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"Total number of unique users in the view"},"4977":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-23","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#the-projection-state-and-progress","content":"(`Main.kt:176-188`)"},"4978":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-24","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#rebuilt-view-and-the-replay-guarantee","content":"When a service restarts, it must rebuild its entire state from the log. The `rebuilt` mode in the testing suite asserts that a restarted projection must return at least as many events as it had before the restart (`check.py:193-207`). A failure is triggered if the `applied` count is less than the previous state or if the `fromReplay` count does not match the expected history, ensuring that the view is not left incomplete (`check.py:209-211`)."},"4979":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-25","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#key-files","content":"File"},"4980":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-26","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#key-files","content":"Lines"},"4981":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-27","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#key-files","content":"What is there"},"4982":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-28","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#key-files","content":"`…/projection/Main.kt`"},"4983":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-29","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over#key-files","content":"`44-77`"},"4984":{"id":"/wiki/dev/state-fold-over-30","page_id":"/wiki/dev/state